hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6051","From Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A woman is shrouded in five garments: a chemise, a head-cover, a rag, and two wraps. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: And how is the rag used? He said: It is made like a waist-wrapper over the chemise."
"6052","From Israil, from Isa b. Abi Azza, he said: I witnessed Amir al-Shabi shroud his daughter in five garments, and he said: A man is in three."
"6053","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: The waist rag is over her chemise."
"6054","From Hisham, from Umm al-Hudhayl, she said: The dead woman is veiled as the living woman is veiled, and a cubits length is let down as chemise from the head-cover over her face."
"6055","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they said: The shroud is from the whole of the wealth."
"6056","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: The shroud and embalming are a debt. And Amr b. Dinar said it."
"6057","From al-Thawri, from Ubayda, from Ibrahim, he said: The shroud is from the whole of the wealth."
"6058","From al-Thawri, from Ubayda, from Ibrahim, he said: Begin with the shroud, then the debt, then the bequest. I said: Then the wage of the grave and washing of the shroud? He said: It is part of the shroud."
"6059","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Said b. Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Said b. al-Musayyib, he said: The shroud is from the whole of the wealth. He said: And Khilas b. Amr said: From the third."
"6060","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Tawus, he said: The shroud is from the whole of the wealth. He said: If the wealth is little, it is in the third."
"6061","From al-Thawri, from Dawud b. Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The shroud of a child is in one garment."
"6062","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Nothing is taken from the hair of the dead nor from his nails. Mamar said, and al-Hasan said: If his hair is long, excessively long, some is taken from it, and his nails likewise."
"6063","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: The dead dies with long hair; is anything taken from it? He said: No; when he dies, then no. Moths flutter from a mans head, then are picked up and gathered and hidden with him when he dies; so nothing is removed from him. As for before he dies, then yes."
"6064","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: When anything of hair was pulled from the head of the dead, it was gathered and hidden with him."
"6065","From al-Thawri, from Yazid b. Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla, he said concerning hair and nail falling from the dead: He said: You put it with him in his shroud."
"6066","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: Aisha saw a woman combing her head, and said: Why do you pluck your dead?"
"6067","From al-Thawri, he said: Hammad was asked about trimming the nails of the dead? He said: What if he were uncircumcised, would you circumcise him?"
"6068","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: It is disliked to shave the pubic hair of the dead. Abd al-Razzaq said, and Mamar said, and al-Hasan said it: If it is excessive, some is taken from it."
"6069","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilaba: Sad b. Malik shaved the pubic hair of a dead person."
"6070","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Who first brought it for the bier of the woman? He said: Asma daughter of Umays; I think she saw that in the land of Abyssinia."
"6071","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr b. Dinar informed me, he said: They, when they carried the woman upon the bed, would turn her and place her between its legs, until Asma informed them."
"6072","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike the yellow velvet for the bier? He said: I do not know. He said: Then the red? He said: Ali b. Abi Talib said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me gold rings, and qassi garments, meaning silk clothes, and purple velvet, and the mithara: a form placed under a man like a carpet, like a small saddle-cloth with red and yellow fringes."
"6073","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: His saying: Ask forgiveness for him, may Allah forgive you? He said: An innovation. And it reached me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said of Dhu al-Bijadayn: Ask forgiveness for him, may Allah forgive you."
"6074","From a man, from Abu Salama b. Abd al-Rahman: Abu Said al-Khudri bequeathed to his family not to carry him upon purple velvet."
"6075","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman b. Harmala, he said: I was with Ibn al-Musayyib at a funeral, and he heard a man saying: Ask Allah for forgiveness. He said: What says this rhymester of theirs? I have enjoined upon my family that no rhymester of theirs chant with me, nor say: Ibn al-Musayyib has died, so witness him; sufficient for me is He who returns me to my Lord; nor walk with me with a censer; for if there is good for me with my Lord, then what is Allahs servant is more fragrant than your perfume. From Mamar, from Ibn Harmala, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"6076","From al-Thawri, from Bukayr al-Amiri, he said: Said b. Jubayr heard a man saying: Ask forgiveness for her. He said: May Allah not forgive you."
"6077","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Hakam b. Aban informed me that he heard Ikrima, freedman of Ibn Abbas, say: A son of Abu Bakr who used to drink drink died, and Abu Hurayra said: Ask forgiveness for him, for forgiveness is but sought for an offender like him."
"6078","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ismail b. Abi Khalid, he said: Abu Juhayfa took the legs of the bed of Amr b. Shurahbil and did not leave it until he reached the grave, saying: O Allah, forgive Abu Maysara."
"6079","From Abu Hammam b. Nafi, he said: I saw Abdullah b. Hasan take the legs of the bed of Tawus, and I saw some of his clothes torn upon him, and his cap fell, yet he did not leave it until he reached the grave. I said to Abu Bakr, he said: Between the two posts of the bier. We said: And where did Tawus die? He said: In Mecca."
"6080","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten with your funerals; if it was righteous, you hasten it to good, and if it was wicked, you rest from it and put it off your necks."
"6081","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: Hasten with your funerals, and do not act Jewish as the People of the Book act Jewish."
"6082","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It was said: Walk briskly with funerals, and do not creep as Jews and Christians creep."
"6083","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad b. Qays, from Nubayh, he said: Abu Said al-Khudri said: There is no funeral but it adjures its bearers; if it was believing and Allah is pleased with it, it says: I adjure you by Allah but that you hasten me; and if it was disbelieving in Allah and Allah is angry with it, it says: I adjure you by Allah but that you return with me. There is nothing but hears it except the two heavy ones; and if man heard it, he would be weakened and panic; khara means weakness and awe."
"6084","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How is walking with a man, do we hasten with him? He said: Yes. I said: Then the woman? He said: Hasten with her also, but less haste than the man; a woman has a form a man has not. It was said: Then what is this walking of yours? He said: And it is."
"6085","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, he said: Nafi attended with Ibn Abbas the funeral of Maymuna, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), at Sarif; Ibn Abbas said: This is the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or he said: This is the husband of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when you lift her bier, do not shake or jolt, and be gentle; for there were nine with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would apportion to eight and not apportion to one. Ata said: The one to whom he did not apportion was Safiyya daughter of Huyayy b. Akhtab. Ibn Jurayj said: And Aisha commanded hastening with funerals."
"6086","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: I witnessed a funeral with Abdullah b. Jafar; he sat in the graveyard, then kept looking at the funeral approaching while they were slow, and said: Glory be to Allah, for what people innovated in funerals; I used to hear a man remind a man and frighten him, saying: Fear Allah, it will soon be rushed with you. No, by Allah, walking with funerals was but rushing."
"6087","From Abdullah b. Said b. Abi Hind, he said: Muhammad b. Amr b. Halhala al-Dayli narrated to me, from a son of Kab b. Malik, from Abu Qatada, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day, and a funeral was passed by him; he said: Relieved, or relieved from. He said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, what is relieved and relieved from? He said: The righteous servant rests from the toil of the world and its worry to the mercy of Allah, and from the wicked servant rest the servants, trees, and beasts."
"6088","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abd al-Rahman b. Yazid, from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla, he said: The soul of the dead is in the hand of the angel; he says: Hear what is praised of you when you are washed and when you are carried; when buried, the earth speaks to him and says: Did you not know I am the house of estrangement, desolation, and worms, so what have you prepared for me?"
"6089","Ibn Jurayj and Dawud b. Qays informed us, from Sad b. Said brother of Yahya, from Amra daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from Aisha: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Breaking the bones of the dead is like breaking them alive."
"6090","From al-Thawri, from Haritha b. Abi al-Rijal, from Amra, from Aisha, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Breaking the bones of the dead is like breaking them alive. Sufyan said: They deem that a sin. Mamar informed us, from Said b. Abd al-Rahman al-Jahshi, from Amra, from Aisha: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of it."
"6091","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar used to walk in front of the funeral bier."
"6092","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri informed me, he said: Salim informed me that his father used to walk in front of the funeral bier."
"6093","From Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: A shaykh of ours called Rabi'ah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hudayr informed us, he said: I saw Ibn al-Khattab beating the people, moving them forward in front of the bier of Zaynab bint Jahsh."
"6094","From Abu Ja'far al-Razi, from Humayd al-Tawil, he said: I heard al-Ayzar asking Anas ibn Malik about walking in front of the bier. Anas said to him: You are only an escort, so walk if you wish in front of it, if you wish behind it, if you wish to its right, and if you wish to its left."
"6095","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never walked in a funeral procession until he died except behind the bier. And by this we abide."
"6096","From al-Thawri, from Urwah ibn al-Harith, from Za'idah ibn Aws al-Kindi, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, he said: I was with Ali at a funeral, and Ali was holding my hand and we were behind it, while Abu Bakr and Umar were walking in front of it. He said: The superiority of one walking behind it over one walking in front of it is like the superiority of the congregational prayer over the prayer of a single person, and they both know of that what I know, but they do not like to cause hardship to the people."
"6097","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walked in front of the bier of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh."
"6098","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Majid al-Hanafi, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, he said: We asked our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about walking with funeral biers, and he said: It is only to be followed and is not a follower, and whoever goes ahead of it is not with it."
"6099","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The angels walk behind it. He said: And it was narrated to me from Ibn Mas'ud that he used to forbid one who attended the funeral from turning away from its path."
"6100","From Husayn ibn Mihran, from al-Mutarrih Abu al-Muhallab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri came to Ali ibn Abi Talib while he was sitting in ihtiba, and greeted him, and he returned the greeting. He said: Abu Hasan, inform me about walking in front of the bier when I attend it, which of that is better, behind it or in front of it? Ali knitted his brows and said: Glory be to Allah, does one like you ask about this? Abu Sa'id said: Yes, by Allah, one like me asks about one like this, and who asks about one like this except one like me? Ali said: By Him Who sent Muhammad with the truth, the superiority of one walking behind it over one walking in front of it is like the superiority of the obligatory prayer over the voluntary. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said to him: Abu Hasan, do you say this by your own opinion or by something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He became angry and said: Glory be to Allah, Abu Sa'id, would I say one like this by my opinion? No, by Allah, rather I heard him say it repeatedly, more than once, not twice, not three times, until he counted seven times. Abu Sa'id said: By Allah, I have not sat in a sitting since I attended a funeral of a man of the Ansar, attended by Abu Bakr, Umar and all the Companions, and I saw Abu Bakr and Umar walking in front of it. Ali laughed and said: You saw them both doing that? Abu Sa'id said: Yes. Ali said: Had one other than you narrated them to me I would not have believed him, but I know lying is not your way. May Allah forgive them both. The best of this ummah is Abu Bakr ibn Abi Quhafah and Umar ibn al-Khattab, then Allah knows best where the good is. And if you saw them both doing that, they both know that the superiority of one walking behind it over one walking in front of it is like the superiority of the obligatory prayer over the voluntary prayer, as they know that after tomorrow is night, and they heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as I heard, but they disliked that the people gather and crowd, so they liked to go ahead and make it easy, and they knew they are taken as example, so for that reason they went ahead."
"6101","Abu Sa'id said: Abu Hasan, tell me, if I attend the funeral, is carrying it obligatory upon whoever attends it? He said: No, but it is good; whoever wishes takes hold and whoever wishes leaves it. When you attend the funeral, put it before you and make it an aim before your eyes, for it is only an admonition, a reminder and a lesson. If it occurs to you to carry it, look at the front of the bed and look at its left side, and put it upon your right shoulder. When you come to the graveyard and pray over it, do not sit, and stand over his grave, for you will see a great matter. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Your brother, your brother, who used to compete with you in the world and contend with you in it, the ease of the land straitened him into palaces, then he enters the belly of a grave leaning on his side. If he does not call you, do not fail to stand until he is lowered into his pit, even if they fight you with fighting."
"6102","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a bier, he has a qirat of reward, and whoever waits for it until it is placed in the lahd has two qirats. It was said: What are the two qirats? He said: Like two great mountains."
"6103","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from one who heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever follows a bier and prays over it has a qirat, and whoever waits for it until its matter is concluded has two qirats, the smaller of them like Uhud."
"6104","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, he said: Ya'la ibn Ata narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, he said: Ibn Umar passed by Abu Hurayrah while he was narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever follows a bier and prays over it has a qirat, and if he witnesses its burial he has two qirats greater than Uhud. Ibn Umar said: Some of your hadith, Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Hurayrah stood and took Ibn Umar by the hand until he went with him to A'ishah. Abu Hurayrah said to her: I ask you by Allah, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever follows a bier and prays over it has a qirat, and if he witnesses its burial he has two qirats, and the qirat is greater than Uhud? A'ishah said: O Allah, yes. Abu Hurayrah said: Planting of young palms and striking deals in the markets never kept me busy; I only sought from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a morsel he would feed me or a word he would teach me. Ibn Umar said to him: You, Abu Hurayrah, were the most attached of us to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the most knowledgeable of us of his hadith."
"6105","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib informed me that Nafi' ibn Jubayr informed him that Abu Hurayrah informed him that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever prays over a bier and follows it has two qirats of reward like Uhud, and whoever prays and does not follow has a qirat like Uhud."
"6106","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Ibn Abbas saw a man leaving when he had prayed over the bier, and Ibn Abbas said to him: This one has left with a qirat of reward."
"6107","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata followed a bier and prayed near the house of Abd al-Aziz. A man said to him: What is there for me of reward? He said: According to how far you followed."
"6108","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, he said: Mujahid was asked: Is voluntary prayer better or following the bier? He said: Rather following the bier."
"6109","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If one follows the bier, may one without ablution carry it? He said: Yes, and more beloved to me is that he be pure, but none prays over it except in ablution, and no menstruating woman prays over it. He is the one who said: I said to him: Going to the rites and the two dispersions, with ablution? He said: With ablution and without ablution."
"6110","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: None prays over a bier without ablution; if he does, he repeats the prayer so long as the dead has not been buried, and when he is buried his prayer has passed."
"6111","From Ma'mar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: None prays over a bier without ablution; if a bier comes to him while he is without ablution and he fears missing it, he performs tayammum and prays over it. And by this we abide."
"6112","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If one attends the bier without ablution, let him perform tayammum. And by this we abide."
"6113","From Ma'mar, he said: A man informed me, from a man who informed him, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz prayed over a bier and kept ordering his family and attendants to perform ablution. Abu Qilabah said: What is this, Commander of the Believers? He said: It reached me, as I think, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever prays over a bier should perform ablution. Abu Qilabah said: It was raised to you in other than its form; he only passed by a bier while the people were in their markets, and they kept following the bier like this, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a bier, let him perform ablution, meaning none prays over it except in ablution. Umar said to him: For one like this I loved your nearness to me."
"6114","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi: If a bier surprises you while you are without ablution, pray over him."
"6115","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: I lived among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); they deemed it desirable to lower the voice at funerals, at recitation of the Qur'an, and at fighting. And by this we abide."
"6116","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he followed the bier, was much silent and spoke much to himself."
"6117","From Muhammad ibn Suqah, from Ibrahim, he said: When they attended the funeral, that was recognized in them for three days."
"6118","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never rode with a funeral bier ever. He said: And I do not know but he said: Nor Abu Bakr, nor Umar."
"6119","From Shu'bah, from Simak ibn Harb, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Ibn al-Dahdah. When he finished the funeral, a horse was brought, and a man held it, and the horse was bare, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode it and made it trot, while we hastened around him."
"6120","From Ma'mar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: They disliked that a rider pass in front of the bier."
"6121","From Fudayl, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked Alqamah: Did they dislike walking in front of the bier? He said: No, but they disliked riding ahead of it, meaning the rider. Ibrahim said: And I saw Alqamah and al-Aswad walking in front of it. And Ibn Abi Awfa said to his guide: Do not put me ahead of it."
"6122","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: We were forbidden from following funeral biers, but it was not made binding upon us."
"6123","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Abi Umayyah, from Amrah, from A'ishah, she said: Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seen the women today, he would have forbidden them from going out, or prohibited going out for them."
"6124","From Umar ibn Dharr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was following a bier, when there was an old woman following it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry until anger was recognized in his face, and he ordered regarding her, so she was turned back. Then he put down the bed and did not say takbir over it until they said: By Him Who sent you with the truth, she has been hidden by the huts of Madinah. He said: Then he said takbir over it."
"6125","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Atiyyah al-Wadi'i, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with a bier and saw a woman, so he ordered regarding her, and she was driven away until he did not see her, then he said takbir."
"6126","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) followed the bier and saw a woman behind it, so he ordered the bier detained, and sent a man who turned the woman back, until when the houses hid her, they walked with it."
"6127","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to lock the doors upon the women until the men took out the biers."
"6128","From al-Thawri, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from Amr ibn Yahya, he said: Women have no share in the funeral."
"6129","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The going out of women with biers? He said: They cause temptation."
"6130","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hibban, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The going out of women with biers is an innovation."
"6131","From al-Thawri, from Muhill, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He was asked: Does a woman pray over biers? He said: She does not pray over them, neither pure nor menstruating."
"6132","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Muwarriq al-Ijli, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with a bier and saw the women, and said: Do you carry him among those who carry him? They said: No. He said: Do you enter him in among those who enter him? They said: No. He said: Do you throw the dust among those who throw? They said: No. He said: Then return back, laden with burden, without reward."
"6133","From Ma'mar, that Umar saw women with a bier and said: Return back, laden with burden, without reward, for by Allah you neither carry nor bury, O harmers of the dead and tempters of the living."
"6134","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, that he used to throw dust in their faces, and if they went on, he returned."
"6135","From al-Thawri, from Khalid ibn Dinar, he said: al-Hasan said: Do not leave a truth for a falsehood."
"6136","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: I went out with Ibn Umar with a bier. When he reached the graveyard he heard a wailer, or a lamenter. He faced her and spoke harshly to her, and said to Mujahid: You went out seeking reward, and this one seeks burden through you. We were forbidden to follow a bier with which there is a lamenter. He said: So he returned, and she returned with him."
"6137","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid, that Ibn Umar followed a bier and saw women following it and screaming. He turned to them and said: Fie upon you, harm to the dead and temptation to the living, three times."
"6138","From his father, he said: A daughter of Wahb died. When the men went out, he closed the door and did not let the women follow her."
"6139","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Amir ibn Rabi'ah informed us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sees a bier, let him stand until it leaves him behind or is put down."
"6140","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Salim, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Amir ibn Rabi'ah al-Adawi said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see the bier, stand until it leaves you behind. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi' informing, from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6141","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions stood for the bier of a Jew until it disappeared."
"6142","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, that Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari and Qays ibn Sa'd used to stand for the bier."
"6143","He said: Marwan was sitting and with him Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and a bier passed, so he stood. Marwan said: What is this? He said: He ordered us, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Then bring it then."
"6144","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Abu Ma'mar, he said: We were with Ali, and a bier passed, and people stood for it. Ali said: Who gave you a fatwa with this? They said: Abu Musa. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only did that once, and he was imitating the People of the Book, then when he was forbidden, he desisted."
"6145","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Qays ibn Mas'ud, from his father, that he attended a funeral with Ali ibn Abi Talib at Kufah, and saw people standing waiting for the bier to be put down, so he gestured to them with a stick he had, or a whip: Sit, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat after he used to stand."
"6146","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Abbas and al-Hasan ibn Ali, a bier passed by them both, and one of them stood and the other sat. The one who stood said: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood? The other said: Yes indeed, and he sat."
"6147","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam, from Ali, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at the grave, then sat."
"6148","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: I was in Madinah and attended the bier of Umm Amr bint al-Zubayr. When prayer was said over her, Ibn al-Musayyab sat, so I stood. Ibn al-Musayyab said to me: Sit. I said: It reached me that Ibn Umar disliked that. He said: Sit, there is no harm upon you."
"6149","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to go ahead of the bier until he came to al-Baqi' and sat, and when he saw it he stood. Nafi' said: I used to screen him so that he would not see it."
"6150","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The standing of one who sees it? He said: Ubayd, freedman of al-Sa'ib, informed me, he said: Ibn Umar followed a bier, and with him Ubayd ibn Umayr, Ibn Abi Aqrab, and I following them. He went ahead of it and sat, until when it came abreast of him, he stood until it left him behind."
"6151","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I heard Abu Qilabah saying: A man standing over the grave until the dead is placed is an innovation."
"6152","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: A bier used to pass by them, and none of them would stand."
"6153","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, that his father used to blame one who stands when a bier passes by him."
"6154","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim, he said: The first who stood for the bier were the Jews."
"6155","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Miswar, that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah would not sit until it was placed in the grave."
"6156","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: He would not sit until the dead was placed in the lahd."
"6157","And he narrates that from Ibn Umar. Ayyub said: I asked Nafi', and he said: When biers were placed upon the ground, Ibn Umar would sit."
"6158","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from al-Minhal, from Zadham, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a bier, and found the grave not yet made into a lahd, so he sat and we sat."
"6159","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Allah ibn Kathir informed me that Mujahid said: It used to be said, when you have prayed over the bier, stand until it is lifted. Then the people altered it and said: Stand until it is put down."
"6160","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata, I said: When you have prayed over a bier and you are not following it? He said: I enter and do not wait for it to be lifted."
"6161","From Ma'mar and others, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: When a bier passes by you, stand; if you follow it, do not sit until it is put down."
"6162","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: When there are men and women, the men are next to the imam, and the women beyond that."
"6163","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Men are before women, and the old before the young."
"6164","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, that he used to pray over biers, placing the men next to the imam and the women in front of that. And by this we abide."
"6165","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn Mawhab, he said: I prayed with Abu Hurayrah and with Ibn Umar over a man and a woman, and he placed the man next to the imam and the woman beyond that, and said takbir four times."
"6166","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The men are next to the imam and the women beyond that."
"6167","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he placed the man next to the imam and the woman in front of that."
"6168","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that he said: When the biers of men and women gather, the men are next to the imam and the women in front of that. From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Ibn al-Musayyab the like of it."
"6169","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, and Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, that Ibn Umar prayed over Umm Kulthum bint Ali ibn Abi Talib and Zayd ibn Umar, and placed Zayd next to him and the woman in front of that."
"6170","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' claims that Ibn Umar prayed over nine biers together, placing the men next to the imam and the women toward the qiblah, arranging them in a row."
"6171","And the bier of Umm Kulthum daughter of Ali, wife of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and a son of hers called Zayd, were placed together, and the imam that day was Sa'id ibn al-As, and among the people were Ibn Abbas, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Sa'id and Abu Qatadah. He placed the boy toward the imam. A man said: I disapproved of that, so I looked at Ibn Abbas, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Sa'id and Abu Qatadah and said: What is this? They said: It is the Sunnah."
"6172","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The men are toward the imam and the women in front of that."
"6173","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me that Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', when he prayed over women and men together, placed the men toward him and the women in front of that."
"6174","From al-Thawri, from Razin, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I saw him come to biers of men and women, and say: Where are the Sa'afiqah, or what do the Sa'afiqah say? Meaning those who criticize. He said: Then he placed the men toward the imam and the women in front of that, some of them behind others. Then he mentioned that Ibn Umar did that with Umm Kulthum and Zayd, and there were men of Banu Hashim. He said: I think he mentioned Hasan and Husayn."
"6175","Ma'mar informed us, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: The men are toward the qiblah and the women toward the imam."
"6176","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: He prays over each one alone."
"6177","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I saw al-Sha'bi pray over the bier of two men, placing one behind the other, then he said: Do with them like this, even if they are ten."
"6178","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When there are freemen and slaves, the freemen are next to the imam."
"6179","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The woman is placed in the prayer place by the feet of the man."
"6180","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', and he was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When he prayed over men and women, he placed the heads of the women toward the knees of the men. He said: And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were with him and did not disapprove of him."
"6181","From al-Awza'i, from Khusayf, he said: One who prayed with Abu al-Darda or Fudalah ibn Ubayd over biers informed us that they both placed the woman by the shoulder of the man."
"6182","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he used to align their heads when he prayed over men and women. And by this we abide. From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Ibn Umar the like of it."
"6183","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Miqdam, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: When there is a bier of a man and a woman, the man is given precedence by the head when they are placed in the prayer place."
"6184","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: The imam stands at the chest of the man and the shoulder of the woman."
"6185","From Ma'mar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: The imam stands at the chest of the man and the shoulder of the woman."
"6186","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from Samurah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a woman and stood at her middle. And by this we abide."
"6187","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust narrated to me, from al-Hasan, that he said: A man stands, when he prays over a woman, at her chest."
"6188","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I saw al-Sha'bi bring forward the biers of two men, placing one behind the other. He said: Do with them like this, even if they are ten."
"6189","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrimah freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the slain of Uhud, praying over them all together, and put toward the qiblah the best reciter of the Qur'an among them. And by this we abide."
"6190","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: You raise your hands at every takbirah of the four takbirs. And by this we abide."
"6191","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The imam raises his hands whenever he says takbir over biers, and the people behind him."
"6192","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, that he used to raise his hands at all the takbirs."
"6193","From a man of the people of al-Jazirah, he said: I heard Nafi' narrating that Ibn Umar used to raise at the four takbirs over the bier."
"6194","From al-Thawri, from one who heard al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim, that he used to raise his hands at the first takbirah in prayer over the dead, then not raise after."
"6195","From Ma'mar, from some of our companions, that Ibn Abbas used to raise his hands at the first takbirah, then not raise after, and he used to say takbir four times. From Ma'mar, he said: It reached him from Ibn Mas'ud the like of that."
"6196","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar prayed over Abu Bakr, and Suhayb prayed over Umar."
"6197","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: al-Zubayr prayed over Umar and buried him, and he had bequeathed to him."
"6198","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', he said: I heard him saying: I prayed over A'ishah, and the imam that day was Abu Hurayrah."
"6199","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: One who used to lead her in prayer in her life prays over her. He said: And that was because a woman died among other people, so Suwayd ibn Ghafalah said that."
"6200","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Their imams used to pray over their biers. He said: When a woman died among other people, the imam of that quarter in which she died prayed over her."
"6201","Al-Thawri informed us, from Salim, from Abu Hazim, he said: I witnessed Husayn, when al-Hasan died, pushing Sa'id ibn al-As in the back of his neck, saying: Go forward, for were it not for the Sunnah I would not have put you forward, and Sa'id was governor over Madinah that day. He said: When they prayed over him, Abu Hurayrah stood and said: Do you grudge the son of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a soil to bury him in? Then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever loves them both has loved me, and whoever hates them both has hated me."
"6202","Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from al-Hasan, he said: The most entitled of people to pray over the woman is the father, then the husband, then the son, then the brother."
"6203","Al-Thawri informed us, from Muhammad ibn Qays al-Asadi, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: I sought permission from the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray over her."
"6204","Ma'mar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata, he said: The husband has more right to pray over the woman than the brother."
"6205","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Yazid ibn Abi Sulayman, from Masruq, from Umar, that he said: The guardian has more right to pray over her."
"6206","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Abd Rabbih, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, he said: A wife of Abu Bakrah died, and her brothers came disputing with him over prayer upon her. Abu Bakrah said: Were I not more entitled to prayer upon her, I would not have disputed you in that. He said: So he went forward and prayed over her, then entered the grave, and was taken out unconscious, and he had that day thirty or forty sons and daughters. They shouted over him and he revived. He said: There is no soul on earth, nor the soul of a fly, more beloved to me to leave my self. It was said to him: Why? He said: Fear that a time reach me in which I do not enjoin good nor forbid evil, so what good is there in me that day?"
"6207","From a man of the people of Madinah, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The most entitled of people to pray over the woman is her husband."
"6208","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: No one led them in prayer over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They used to enter in groups, men, women and children, into the house in which he was and the chamber, supplicating, then going out, and others entering, until the people finished."
"6209","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken on Monday, and was not buried that day nor that night, until the end of Tuesday. He said: And he was washed with his shirt upon him, and shrouded in three garments: two Sahari garments and a Hibarah cloak, and prayer was said over him without an imam, and Umar ibn al-Khattab called among the people: Leave the bier and its people. And a lahd was made for him, and bricks were placed over his lahd."
"6210","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me that Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', when he buried men and women together, placed the man in the grave toward the qiblah and placed the woman behind him in the grave. Sulayman said: If they are two men in one grave, the imam says takbir. He said: The elder is the imam of the younger."
"6211","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from a man, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud used to bury two men and three in one grave, asking which of them knew most Qur'an, and putting him forward toward the qiblah. Al-Zuhri mentioned it from Ibn Abi al-Su'ayr, from Jabir."
"6212","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to put forward in the grave toward the qiblah the best reciter among them, then the eldest."
"6213","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Four, apart from the people, undertook the washing of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his burial and his covering: Ali, al-Abbas, al-Fadl, and Salih Shuqran, freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And they made a lahd for him, and set up bricks over him upright."
"6214","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, that he made a lahd for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then set up bricks over his lahd."
"6215","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from his father, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, there were in Madinah two men, a man who made lahds and a man who made split graves. The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered and said: O Allah, choose for him. He said: The one who made lahds appeared, so he made a lahd for him."
"6216","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: There were in Madinah two men, one of them made lahds for graves and the other split. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, they said: Whichever of them comes, we order him to do his work. The one who made lahds came, so they ordered him, and he made a lahd for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6217","From al-Thawri, from Salim, from Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman Abu al-Yaqzan, from Zadham, from Jarir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lahd is for us, and the split grave is for others."
"6218","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to deem the lahd desirable, disliked the split grave, disliked payment for the grave, deemed bricks and reeds desirable, and disliked that when it was leveled over the dead, the guardian stand over his grave to be condoled for him."
"6219","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed us, from his father, that the one who made the lahd for the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Abu Talhah, and that the one who cast the velvet cloak was the freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ibn Shuqran."
"6220","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il ibn Muslim informed me, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a worn-out velvet cloak upon which he used to ride in his life was spread in his grave. We said to Abu Bakr: If the people do that? He said: No indeed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not like others."
"6221","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me that a Fadakiyyah velvet cloak was spread in the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6222","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah son of the brother of Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from his uncle, he said: Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), died at Sarif. I took my cloak and spread it beneath her, but Ibn Abbas took it and threw it away."
"6223","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from more than one of their companions, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a brick was placed as pillow, his head put to it supporting him, and it is not placed under his cheek. We said to Abu Bakr: A sound brick or a fragment? He said: Rather a brick."
"6224","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Bakr and Ali, that a lahd was made for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and bricks were laid across him and set up."
"6225","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced the death of al-Najashi to his Companions while they were in Madinah, and they lined up behind him, and he prayed over him and said takbir four times. And by this we abide."
"6226","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a woman and said takbir over her four times."
"6227","From al-Thawri, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Wa'il, he said: They used to say takbir in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seven, five and four times, until the time of Umar, when he gathered them and asked them, and each man of them informed him of what he had seen, so he gathered them upon four takbirs, like the longest prayer, meaning Zuhr."
"6228","From al-Thawri, from Razin, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit said takbir over his mother four takbirs, and did not envy her any good."
"6229","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, he said: Umar said takbir over Zaynab bint Jahsh four takbirs, and asked the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who should enter her into her grave? They said: Whoever used to see her in her life."
"6230","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umayr ibn Sa'id, he said: Ali said takbir over Yazid ibn al-Mukaffaf al-Nakha'i four times."
"6231","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil saying: Ali prayed over Sahl ibn Hunayf and said takbir over him six times."
"6232","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Ali said takbir over a bier five times."
"6233","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: Each has been done, then the people gathered upon four takbirs."
"6234","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to gather the people with al-hamd, and say takbir over biers three times."
"6235","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil narrated to me that Ali prayed over Sahl ibn Hunayf and said takbir over him six times, then turned to us and said: He is a Badri."
"6236","Al-Sha'bi said: Alqamah came from Syria and said to Ibn Mas'ud: Your brothers in Syria say five takbirs over their funerals; if only you would fix a number for us to follow you on. Abdullah bowed his head a while, then said: Look at your funerals and say over them whatever takbir your imams say; no fixed time and no fixed number."
"6237","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq al-Hajari: I saw Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa pray over a daughter of his and say four takbirs, then he stood a while and they prompted him with tasbih. He said: You think I will say five, yet I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say four takbirs. Then he rode with her and kept telling his guide: Do not place me ahead of her. The women began to weep, so he said: Do not eulogize, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid eulogies. Ibn Uyaynah said: Ibn Abi Awfa was blind, and they regarded his standing after the fourth takbir as supplication for the dead, and most people follow that."
"6238","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: The takbir over a man and a woman is four. I said: By night and day? He said: Yes. I said: If two men are placed together? He said: Four takbirs are said over them both. The questioner said: Some people say three, like Maghrib is three. He said: We never heard of that."
"6239","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata told me he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Today a righteous man from Abyssinia, Ashamah, has died; come, pray over him. We lined up, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us with us. Abd al-Razzaq said: The meaning of Ashamah in Arabic is Ata (gift)."
"6240","From Ibn Jurayj: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jabr told me he heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed at the funeral place and said four takbirs, then said: Do you know over whom I prayed? They said: No. He said: Over Ashamah."
"6241","From Ibn Jurayj: Al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhubab told me they unanimously agreed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the Negus at Baqi al-Musalla. Abd al-Razzaq said: Al-Thawri, when he said four takbirs over funerals, would salute and not wait for a fifth, and I follow that."
"6242","From Ibn Jurayj: Abu Bakr told me, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Umm Kulthum, the sister of Sawda bint Zam'a, who died in Makkah, and he prayed over her at Baqi al-Musalla and said four takbirs over her."
"6243","From Ibn Jurayj: Musa ibn Uqba told me, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar would prolong the standing in prayer over funerals, and the imam would say four takbirs."
"6244","Ma'mar told us, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: When the imam says takbir over a funeral and then another bier is brought, he says four over it, so it is four for the second and five for the first. Ibrahim disliked that the last contact with the dead be [lacuna in source], or that a rider pass in front of the funeral, or that a man stand between the two poles of the bier at its front or rear, or that the family of the dead pass close before or behind the funeral to glorify the dead. If a funeral surprises him while he is without wudu, he does tayammum and prays over it. If he misses any takbir, he hastens before it is lifted and says what he missed."
"6245","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: If any of the takbir with the imam escapes you, say what escaped you."
"6246","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, and al-Thawri, from Hammad, and Mughira, from Ibrahim: If any of the takbir escapes you, hasten before it is lifted. And we adopt this."
"6247","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: If he misses part of the takbir over the funeral, he makes up what he missed."
"6248","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Amr, from al-Hasan: Over the funeral, if he missed any of the takbir, he would not make it up."
"6249","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from al-Harith ibn Zayd: If you come when the imam has said takbir over the dead and you stand in the row but did not say takbir until they say takbir, then say takbir with them."
"6250","Ma'mar told us, from Qatada, from Anas, that he said three takbirs over a funeral, then left forgetfully, and he talked and the people talked. They said: O Abu Hamza, you said three. He said: Line up. They did, and he said the fourth."
"6251","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: If you pray over a funeral, whatever passes before you does not harm you. Meaning: what breaks the prayer? Ma'mar says: Al-Hasan said it too."
"6252","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in supplication for the dead: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our young and our old, our males and our females, our absent and our present. O Allah, whom You keep alive among us, keep him upon Islam, and whom You take among us, take him upon faith. And we adopt this."
"6253","From Abu Ishaq, from a man of Muzayna, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), regarding the words over the dead: O Allah, Your servant and son of Your servant, You created him, You took his soul, You guided him to Islam, and You know his secret and his open; we have come to intercede for him, so forgive him."
"6254","From al-Thawri, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn al-Musayyib: Umar ibn al-Khattab would say thrice over funerals: O Allah, Your servant so-and-so has entered the morning, if it was morning, and if it was evening he said: Your servant has entered the evening, freed from the world and left it to its people, in need of You while You are free of him; he testified that there is no god but You and that Muhammad is Your servant and messenger, so forgive him and overlook him. Ma'mar mentioned it from Qatada."
"6255","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Ali, that he would say over the dead: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, unite our hearts, mend our relations, set our hearts upon the hearts of our best. O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, return him to better than he was in, O Allah, Your pardon. When news of an absent man came to him, he said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. O Allah, raise his rank among the guided, succeed him in his legacy among the survivors, and we count him with You, O Lord of the worlds. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor test us after him."
"6256","From Ibn Jurayj: I heard Nafi claim that Ibn Umar would say in prayer over the funeral: O Allah, bless him, send prayers upon him, forgive him, and bring him to the Pond of Your Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). From Dawud ibn Qays, that he heard Nafi narrate similar from Ibn Umar; meaning bless him: admit him to Paradise."
"6257","From Malik, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from his father, that he asked Abu Hurayra: How do you pray over funerals? Abu Hurayra said: By Allah, I will tell you: I follow it with its family; when they place it, I say takbir, praise Allah, send prayers upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then I say: O Allah, Your servant, son of Your servant, son of Your maid, he testified that there is no god but You and that Muhammad is Your servant and messenger, and You know him best. O Allah, if he was good, increase in his good, and if he was bad, overlook him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor test us after him."
"6258","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata told me, and I asked him about prayer over funerals, and he told me from Abu Salih al-Zayyat: Begin with prayers upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then say: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, mend our relations, unite our hearts, set our hearts upon the hearts of our best. O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, return him to better than he was in, make today the best day that came upon him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor test us after him."
"6259","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Talha ibn Ubaydullah ibn Awf: I prayed with Ibn Abbas over a funeral, and he recited the Fatiha of the Book. I asked him about it, and he said: It is part of the perfection of the Sunna, or: It is part of the Sunna."
"6260","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: I heard Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrate to Ibn al-Musayyib: The Sunna in prayer over funerals is to say takbir, then recite the Mother of the Quran, then send prayers upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then sincerely supplicate for the dead, and not recite except in the first takbir, then salute quietly to his right. Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Shihab told me: The recitation in prayer over the dead is in the first takbir."
"6261","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father: I gathered forty books on prayer over funerals, and kept one book in which: He says takbir, then recites the Mother of the Quran, then sends prayers upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then says: O Allah, Your servant so-and-so, You created him; if You punish him it is for his sin, and if You forgive him, You are the Forgiving, the Merciful. O Allah, raise his spirit to the heaven, widen the earth from his body. O Allah, light for him in his grave, expand for him in Paradise, succeed him in his family. O Allah, do not misguide us after him, do not deprive us of his reward, and forgive us and him. Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Mujahid. He said: Ma'mar ordered me, so I asked Ibn Mujahid about this hadith, then Ma'mar asked me about it, and I narrated it to him."
"6262","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan: He would recite the Mother of the Quran in all the takbirs, saying: O Allah, Your servant so-and-so, magnify his reward and his light, join him to his Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), expand for him in his grave. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor misguide us after him."
"6263","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that he would recite the Fatiha of the Book in every takbir."
"6264","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He would not recite in any of the takbirs, and he would say: O Allah, forgive the believing men and believing women, and the Muslim men and Muslim women, unite their hearts, set their hearts upon the hearts of their best. O Allah, raise his rank among the guided, succeed him in his legacy among the survivors. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor misguide us after him."
"6265","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: I asked him: Does one recite over the dead when he prays over him? He said: No."
"6266","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim, from al-Sha'bi: The first takbir over the dead is praise of Allah, the second is prayers upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the third is supplication for the dead, and the fourth is salutation."
"6267","From al-Thawri, from Mansur: I said to Ibrahim: Is there anything fixed over the dead? He said: I do not know it. Sufyan said: It reached us that Ibrahim said: Upon him is supplication and seeking forgiveness."
"6268","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib: We do not know in prayer over the dead any fixed recitation or supplication as set formula."
"6269","From Ibn Jurayj: It was narrated to me from Abu Hurayra, Abu al-Darda, Anas ibn Malik, and Ibn Abbas, that they would recite the Mother of the Quran and supplicate and seek forgiveness after each of the three takbirs, then say the fourth takbir and leave, without reciting."
"6270","From Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata: Is it disliked to join another with the dead in prayer over the funeral? He said: That never reached us."
"6271","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Huwayrith, from Ibn Abbas, that when he prayed over a funeral he said: O Allah, make him a forerunner for us, make Paradise the appointment between us and him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor misguide us after him."
"6272","From a man of Madinah, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah, from Abu Bakr ibn al-Munkadir, from Ata ibn Yasar: Zayd ibn Thabit would say over the funeral: O Allah, Your servant and son of Your servant, You gave him life as You willed, took him when You willed, and will raise him when You willed. O Allah, if he was pure, purify him, and if he was sinful, overlook him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor misguide us after him. O Allah, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith — the verse."
"6273","From Ma'mar: When you say takbir behind the imam over the funeral, let yourself hear it."
"6274","From Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyaynah: Ibn Tawus told us from his father, and I said to him: The best said over the dead is seeking forgiveness."
"6275","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf: When the imam prays over the funeral, he salutes quietly to his right. And we adopt this."
"6276","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid: Ibn Abbas saluted a light salutation over the funeral."
"6277","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: The imam salutes over the funeral to his right with a light salutation. Al-Thawri said: Al-Shaybani told me from Abd al-Malik ibn Iyas from Ibrahim the same."
"6278","From al-Thawri, from Mansur ibn Hayyan, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: He salutes a light salutation."
"6279","From Ma'mar: It reached me from Abu Hurayra that he saluted over a funeral until those next to him heard. Ibn Jurayj said it from Abu Hurayra."
"6280","From Ata: The imam salutes over the funeral as he salutes in prayer, and those behind him salute."
"6281","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that when he prayed over a funeral, he saluted until those next to him heard."
"6282","From Ibn Jurayj: Musa told me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that when he finished prayer over the funeral, he saluted to his right."
"6283","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that Ibn Sirin prayed over a funeral and let them hear the salutation."
"6284","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Put into the grave as many as you wish. From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim the same. And we adopt this."
"6285","From al-Thawri and others, from Salih the freedman of al-Taw'ama, from Ibn Abbas: Those who descended into the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were Ali, al-Fadl, and Shuqran."
"6286","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi: Ibn Abi Marhab told me: As if I see them in the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), four: Ali, al-Fadl, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Usama — or Abbas."
"6287","From Ibn Jurayj: One I trust told me that those who descended into the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were Ali and al-Fadl, and Ali took charge of its lower side in the grave, and a man of the Ansar descended with them. The Ansar said: We had a share in his life, so give us a share in his death, so they let that Ansari down with them. It reached me that he was Khawli ibn Aws."
"6288","From Ibn Jurayj: I asked Ata about the words when the dead is lowered into the grave. He said: A son of Ubayd ibn Umayr died, and when he took him from above the grave I heard Ubayd say: In the name of Allah, upon the religion of Ibrahim, upright, and he was not of the polytheists. Our prayer, our rites, our life, and our death belong to Him, Lord of the worlds, no partner for Him, thus we are commanded, and we are of the Muslims. And we adopt this."
"6289","Ma'mar told us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Miqsam, and from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam: It was said over the dead in the grave when lowered: In Your name, O Allah, and in Your path, and upon the religion of Your Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). O Allah, accept him from You with good acceptance, bring him to the best return. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward nor test us after him."
"6290","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid: When you lower the dead into his lahd, say: In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). O Allah, expand for him in his grave, light his grave for him, join him to his Prophet, while You are pleased with him, not angry."
"6291","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Murra, from Khaythama: They liked to say over the dead: In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). O Allah, expand for him in his grave, light his grave for him, join him to his Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while You are pleased with him, not angry."
"6292","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Murra, from Khaythama: They liked to say over the dead: In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). O Allah, protect him from the torment of the Fire, the torment of the grave, and the evil of Satan."
"6293","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim: Al-Nazzal ibn Sabra said: When you enter me into my pit, say: O Allah, bless this house and bless its entrant."
"6294","From Isra'il, from Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, that when he entered the dead into his grave he would say: In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we adopt this."
"6295","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, when he entered the dead into the lahd, would say: In the name of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with certainty in resurrection after death."
"6296","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq: I attended the funeral of al-Harith al-A'war al-Kharifi, who was of the companions of Ali and Ibn Mas'ud, and I saw Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Ansari uncover the bier cloth from him when he was entered into the grave, and say: He is but a man. And he said: I saw dharīra perfume on his shroud, and he drew him from the foot side of the grave. Then he said: Like this."
"6297","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hayyan, from his father, from Rabi ibn Khuthaym: Do not inform anyone of me, and slide me to my Lord sliding. From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubruma, from Rabi ibn Khuthaym the same."
"6298","From Isra'il, from Isa ibn Abi Azza: I saw Amir enter his daughter into the grave from the feet side."
"6299","From Ibn Jurayj: Imran ibn Musa told me: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was slid from the head side, and the people after him."
"6300","From Ibn Jurayj, from several Medinans, from Muhammad ibn Amr, Sa'id ibn Khalid, Yahya ibn Rabi'a, and Musa ibn Uqba, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was slid from the head side, and Abu Bakr and Umar; the practice before them remained so, and likewise the woman. Abu Bakr said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad told me it."
"6301","From al-Thawri: It was narrated to me from Ibrahim: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was entered into the grave from the qibla side."
"6302","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Umar ibn Sa'd, that Ali took Yazid ibn al-Mukaffaf from the qibla side. And we adopt this."
"6303","From al-Thawri, from Imran ibn Abi Ata: I saw Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya, when Ibn Abbas died, take him from the qibla side when he entered him into the grave."
"6304","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa: Asma bint Abi Bakr bequeathed that no hanut perfume be sprinkled on her bier cloth."
"6305","From al-Thawri, from Talha ibn Yahya al-Qurashi: I saw Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz forbid dharīra being sprinkled over the bier."
"6306","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq: Al-Harith al-Kharifi died, and I saw Abdullah ibn Yazid say: Scrape off this cloth, for he is but a man — meaning the cloth screen over the grave."
"6307","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Sha'bi: Zayd ibn Malik said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a cloth, and it was screened over the grave when he lowered Sa'd ibn Mu'adh into it."
"6308","Sa'id said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended into the grave of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, with Usama ibn Zayd, and a cloth was screened over the grave, and I was among those holding the cloth. And we adopt this."
"6309","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: The Muhajirun would make a lahd for their dead, set bricks upright over the lahd, then pour soil over them. And we adopt this."
"6310","From Ma'mar, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an: When Ibn Abbas buried Zayd ibn Thabit, he poured soil over him, then said: Thus knowledge is buried. Ali ibn Zayd said: I narrated it to Ali ibn al-Husayn, and he said: By Allah, with Ibn Abbas much knowledge was buried."
"6311","From al-Thawri, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Umayr ibn Sa'd, that Ali poured soil over Yazid ibn al-Mukaffaf. He said: It is he, or another, thrice. [source phrasing fragmented]"
"6312","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: The Prophet passed by a grave that had been sprinkled with water, and said: Had we prayed over this? They said: No, so he prayed over him."
"6313","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad and al-Aslami: From his father: Sprinkling was in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6314","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by al-Baqi and saw a moist grave, and asked about it. They said: O Messenger of Allah, this is al-Suwayda who was among Banu Ghanm; she died and was buried at night. He said: So he prayed over her. Abd al-Razzaq said: As for when a close one of mine dies and I miss prayer over him, it becomes binding to pray over him; as for people generally, supplication is dearer to me."
"6315","From Ibn Jurayj: Abu Bakr told me, from several, that the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had its mound raised a span, and its back made humped without a sharp hump."
"6316","Ma'mar told us, from Ayyub, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: The wall over the grave of the Prophet fell, then it was screened and built. I said to the one who screened it: Lift the side of the screen until I look at it; on it were pebbles and sand as if from courtyard sand."
"6317","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Wa'il, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, that he said: Do not make my mound long. Abd al-Razzaq said: Ma'mar said in his hadith: I saw the Muhajirun dislike that."
"6318","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Wa'il: Ali said to Abu Hayyaj: I dispatch you as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched me: Do not leave a raised grave but level it — meaning the graves of the Muslims — nor an image in a house but efface it?"
"6319","Ibn Jurayj told us: Abu al-Zubayr told me he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid a man sitting on the grave, plastering it, and building over it."
"6320","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Uthman ordered the leveling of graves. He said: But raise slightly from the ground. He said: They passed by the grave of Umm Umar and the daughter of Uthman; he ordered it, and it was leveled."
"6321","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Hasan, that he disliked squaring the grave, meaning the head of the grave."
"6322","Al-Thawri said, and some of our companions told me, from al-Sha'bi: The graves of the people of Uhud were piled humps."
"6323","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from a man called Abu Na'ama: I attended Musa ibn Talha, and he witnessed a funeral and said: Gather the graves in a gathering. Meaning: Do not raise and do not hump."
"6324","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from a man I think Thumama ibn Shufayy, that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he attended his burial, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Lighten for your companion — meaning: Do not pile much soil over his grave."
"6325","From Ma'mar, from Tawus, from his father: He disliked building over the grave, or plastering it, or relieving oneself near it, and he would say: Do not take the graves of your brothers as privies."
"6326","From al-Thawri: If a grave in Makkah passes ten years, do with it as you like — a house, a mosque, plowing, or whatever; as for your lands, twenty years."
"6327","Al-Nu'man ibn Abi Shayba told us: An uncle of mine died in al-Janad, and I entered with my father to Ibn Tawus. He said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, do you think I should plaster my brother's grave? He laughed and said: Glory be to Allah, O Abu Shayba, it is better for you not to know his grave, except to come to it, seek forgiveness for him, and supplicate for him. Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the graves of the Muslims to be built over, plastered, or sown? The best of your graves are those unknown."
"6328","From al-Nu'man: I heard Tawus asked about a rakiyya among the graves, and he disliked drinking from it or ablution. I said: What is rakiyya? He said: Some say it is a well, and some say it is a pond among the graves. I said: Which do you say? He said: We say it is a well. I said: Do you dislike ablution from it? He said: Yes. I said: Why? He said: Because when rain abounds, the graves flood, so I dislike ablution from it."
"6329","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Rashid ibn Sa'd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade plastering graves, crowning them, and writing on them. Al-Bajali said: Taklil means raising; others said: Taklil is coating atop like plaster."
"6330","Ma'mar told us, from Zayd ibn Aslam: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over a grave being dug and said: Do like that. Then he said: I do not mean it avails him anything, but Allah loves, when a deed is done, that it be perfected. Ma'mar said: It reached me in another hadith: It did not avail him anything, but it comforts the hearts of his family."
"6331","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Ala, from Makhul: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat at his son's grave, he saw a gap and said to the digger: Bring me a mud-brick to block it; it neither harms nor benefits, but it comforts the eye of the living."
"6332","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from a man of the Ansar: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat at a grave while it was being lahded, and said to the one making the lahd: Widen from the feet side."
"6333","Ma'mar and Ibn Uyaynah told us, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal: Hisham ibn Amir told me: My father was killed on the day of Uhud, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dig, widen, do well, bury two and three in a grave, and put foremost the one with most Quran. My father was the third of three, and was the one with most Quran, so he was put first."
"6334","From Ibn Jurayj: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Mulayka say: I saw Ibn Abbas, when they finished the grave of Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib, with the people with him; Ibn Abbas stood over it and supplicated for him. He said: Did you hear anything of his words? He said: No."
"6335","From Ibn Jurayj: Abu Bakr told me, from several of his townsmen, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over the grave of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh when he finished it, supplicated for him, and prayed over him. From there it was taken."
"6336","Ma'mar told us, from Ayyub: Ibn al-Munkadir stood over a grave after it was finished and said: O Allah, make him firm; he is now questioned."
"6337","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Mudrik al-Ashja'i: When Umar leveled the grave over the dead, he said: O Allah, family, wealth, and kin have surrendered him to You, and his sin is great, so forgive him."
"6338","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umayr ibn Sa'id: Ali said four takbirs over Yazid ibn al-Mukaffaf and sat at the grave while he was buried. He said: O Allah, Your servant and son of Your servant has come down to You today, and You are the best to come to. O Allah, widen his entrance, forgive his sin, for we know of him only good, and You know him best. And we adopt this."
"6339","He said: It reached me that Ibn Abbas, when he finished burying Maymuna, stood over the grave, supplicated a while, then left."
"6340","From Ibn Jurayj: It was narrated to me from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa al-Aslami: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mazabi as graves; mazabi are those taken for hunting. [source term obscure]"
"6341","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata said to me: It is disliked to perform ablution over graves or sit on them. I said to him: Do I step over it? He said: I dislike it. He said: When we reach the grave of one of them, we tread on it. [source phrasing fragmented]"
"6342","From Ibn Jurayj: From a man, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: He came to the graveyard of Makkah, and it was said to him: Do you tread over the grave? Where do I tread? Here? And he pointed to Thaniyyat al-Madaniyyin."
"6343","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Hurayra: To sit on an ember that burns my cloak, then my shirt, then my waist-wrapper, then reaches my skin, is dearer to me than to sit on the grave of a Muslim man."
"6344","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Zayd, from Talq ibn Habib: Ibn Mas'ud said: To tread on the embers of ghada wood is dearer to me than to tread on the grave of a Muslim man. From Ja'far, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Salim al-Barrad, from Ibn Mas'ud the same."
"6345","From Ibn Jurayj: Isma'il ibn Kathir told me he was with Sa'id ibn Jubayr at a funeral, and Sa'id carried: He began with the front of the bier at the head, put it on his right shoulder, then returned to its end at the feet and carried it on his left shoulder, then came to its end at the head and put it on his left shoulder, then left to his right, and said: Thus is the carrying of funerals."
"6346","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub: I saw him carry a funeral: He began with the front of the bier and put it on his right shoulder, then did as Ibn Jurayj mentioned from Sa'id ibn Jubayr. Ayyub said: If you carry the first thus, carry after as you wish."
"6347","From al-Thawri, from Jabir: One who heard Ibn Umar told me: Begin with the right sides; and he would begin with his hand then his feet."
"6348","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Mansur, from Ubayd ibn Nistas, from Abu Ubayda, from Ibn Mas'ud: If one of you follows a funeral, let him take all its sides, for it is Sunna, then volunteer after or leave."
"6349","From al-Thawri, from Abbad ibn Mansur: Abu al-Muhazzim told me, from Abu Hurayra, that he said: Whoever carries the funeral by its four sides fulfills his duty."
"6350","From Husayn ibn Mihran, from al-Muttarih Abu al-Muhallab, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umama: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said to Ali: O Abu Hasan, if I attend a funeral, is carrying obligatory on attender? He said: No, but it is good; who wishes takes, who wishes leaves. If you attend a funeral, put it before you, make it before your eyes, for it is admonition, reminder, and lesson. If you wish to carry, look to the front of the bier and its left side, and put it on your right shoulder."
"6351","From Hushaym: Ya'la ibn Ata told me, from al-Azdi: I saw Ibn Umar at a funeral carry by the four sides of the bier. He began with its right sides, then withdrew from it, and was from it at the distance of a dog-driver."
"6352","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi: Ibn Umar would not stand when he witnessed until he was permitted, when he prayed over it."
"6353","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, when he prayed over a funeral, would not leave until he was permitted."
"6354","From Ma'mar, from Amir ibn Abd al-Wahid, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Abu Hurayra, and from al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i: Two commanders yet not commanders: A man with a funeral who prayed over it has no right to return until he seeks the permission of its guardian, and a menstruating woman — her companions should not depart until they seek permission. Ma'mar said in his hadith: Abu Hurayra would not leave until he sought permission. Ma'mar said: It reached me from Umar and Ali that they would not leave until they sought permission."
"6355","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq: Ibn Mas'ud said: If you prayed over a funeral, you have fulfilled your duty, so leave it and its family. He would leave without seeking their permission."
"6356","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he would leave and not wait for their permission. And we adopt this."
"6357","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Zayd ibn Thabit: If you prayed over the funeral, you have fulfilled your duty, so leave it to its family."
"6358","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, that they would leave and not wait for their permission."
"6359","From Ibn Jurayj and others, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had, that he saw al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr following a funeral, and they heard the call before finishing, so they stood when they heard the call before finishing it."
"6360","From Ibn Jurayj: It was narrated to me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz went with a funeral; when it was placed in the grave, he left without seeking permission."
"6361","From al-Thawri and others, from al-Salt ibn Bahram, from al-Harith ibn Wahb: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My nation remains on a remnant of its religion so long as they do not entrust funerals to their families."
"6362","From Ibn Jurayj: Umar ibn Ata ibn Waraz told me, from Ikrima the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that he said: Every man is buried in the soil he was created from."
"6363","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Yahya ibn Bahman: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bodies are buried where souls are seized — meaning when one dies, he is not carried from village to another; he is buried in his people's graveyard; as for the very spot where he dies, that was done only for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6364","From al-Aslami: Nuh ibn Abi Bilal told me, from Abu Sulayman al-Hudhali, from Abu Hurayra: No child is born but Allah sends an angel who takes soil from the earth and places it on his navel cut; in it is his healing, and his grave is where the soil was taken from."
"6365","From Ibn Jurayj: My father told me: The companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not know where to bury the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until Abu Bakr said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No prophet is buried except where he dies. So they moved his bedding and dug for him under his bedding."
"6366","From Ibn Jurayj: I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka say: A'isha said: If I had attended Abd al-Rahman — meaning her brother — he would be buried only where he died. He died at al-Hubshi and was buried in upper Makkah, and al-Hubshi is near Makkah."
"6367","From Ibn Jurayj, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman: His mother Safiyya told him: I condoled A'isha over her brother, and she said: May Allah have mercy on my brother; what I hold most against his affair is that he was not buried where he died."
"6368","From Ibn Jurayj: Kathir ibn Kathir told me, from his mother A'isha bint Abi Aqrab, from A'isha the Mother of the Believers, that she said: If I had attended him — meaning her brother — he would have been buried under his bedding."
"6369","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abu Rafi: A man would tend the mosque, cleaning what he found in it, and he died. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about him after some days. They said: He died, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Why did you not inform me? My prayer over them is light in their graves."
"6370","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr died six miles from Makkah, so we carried him to Makkah and buried him. A'isha came to us after, blamed us for that, then said: Where is my brother's grave? We showed her, and she was placed in her hawdaj at his grave and prayed over him."
"6371","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a funeral after it was buried."
"6372","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: A black woman would stay in the mosque and she died, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over her after she was buried."
"6373","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf: A woman of the Awali fell ill, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would ask about her, and he was the best in visiting the sick. He said: If she dies, inform me of her. She died at night, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke, asked, they told him, and that they buried her at night. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to her grave, prayed over her, and said four takbirs."
"6374","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Hanash ibn al-Mu'tamir: People came after prayer over Sahl ibn Hunayf, so Ali ordered Qaraza al-Ansari to lead them and pray over him after he was buried."
"6375","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: Prayer over the dead is not repeated."
"6376","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi: When Ibn Umar reached a funeral already prayed over, he supplicated and left, and did not repeat the prayer."
"6377","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: Ibn Umar came after his brother Asim died, asked about him, and said: Where is my brother's grave? They showed him, so he came to it and supplicated for him. And we adopt this."
"6378","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan: When the prayer escaped him, he would not pray over it. [source text terse]"
"6379","Ma'mar said: When Qatada missed prayer over the funeral, he would pray over it."
"6380","From Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata: Burial at night? He said: No harm in it."
"6381","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrating: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave a sermon one day and mentioned a man among his Companions who had died, was shrouded in an inadequate shroud, and was buried at night. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man be buried at night until the funeral prayer is performed over him, except when people are compelled to do so. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you shrouds his brother, let him make his shroud good."
"6382","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was buried at night."
"6383","From Ibn Jurayj and others, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: We did not know of the burial of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until we heard the sound of spades at the end of the night."
"6384","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: that Abu Bakr was buried at night, and the funeral prayer was performed over him in the mosque."
"6385","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sad informed me, from Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq: that Umar buried Abu Bakr after the last Isha prayer, after he had prayed it."
"6386","From Ibn Jurayj and Amr ibn Dinar, that Hasan ibn Muhammad informed him: that Fatimah, daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was buried at night. He said: Ali hastened with her away from Abu Bakr, lest he pray over her; there was something between them. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Hasan ibn Muhammad, the same, except that he said: she had bequeathed that to him."
"6387","From Ma'mar, from Urwah, from Aishah: that Ali buried Fatimah at night, and did not inform Abu Bakr of her."
"6388","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from al-Shabi, he said: Shurayh used to bury at night."
"6389","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim al-Ahwal, he said: Shurayh used to deliberately attend to his dead at night, and when morning came he was asked about him, and he would say: He has become calm, and we hope he has found rest."
"6390","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Hasan ibn Muslim and others among his companions saying: There was a man of Najd who, when he supplicated, raised his voice, when he prayed, raised his voice, and when he recited, raised his voice. So Abu Dharr complained of him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this Bedouin has annoyed me; if he supplicates he raises his voice, and if he recites he raises his voice. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him, for he is tender-hearted. Abu Dharr said: When the expedition of Tabuk came, I saw a night fire and said: I shall go to this fire and see what is there. There was a funeral being prepared, and a man in the grave saying: Bring your companion near to me, bring your companion near to me. And in the grave was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the funeral was that Bedouin."
"6391","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I said to Nafi: Did Ibn Umar pray over funerals after Asr and Fajr? He said: Yes, as long as they prayed them in their time. From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the same."
"6392","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah: they used to pray over funerals after Asr and Fajr as long as they were within the time."
"6393","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar: that he disliked that funeral prayer be performed when the sun rose, until it had risen somewhat."
"6394","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Ibn Umar, that he said: Take out the funerals before the sun declines toward sunset."
"6395","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that Ibn Umar said on the day the funeral of Rafi ibn Khadij was placed at Baqi al-Gharqad, that they should pray over it after Fajr before sunrise: so Ibn Umar shouted to the people: Do you not fear Allah? It is not proper for you to pray over funerals after Fajr until the sun rises, nor after Asr until the sun sets. So the people stopped and did not pray over it until the sun rose."
"6396","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The prayer over the funeral at the time in which prayer is disliked? He said: It is disliked."
"6397","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in praying over the funeral as long as the sun has not set."
"6398","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Yasar informed me, he said: I was at Madinah with Ibn Umar during the fitnah, and Abbas ibn Sahl, a man of the Ansar, came and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, Aqil ibn Abi Talib has been placed at the door of the mosque to be prayed over, and that was after Asr. He said: O Ibn Yasar, see, has the sun set? He said: No, so he refused to stand. He said: Then he returned to him and said: See, has the sun set? So I looked and said: No, so he refused to pray over him. He said: So they took him and prayed over him, though they had wanted Ibn Umar to lead them, while Ibn al-Zubayr was then at Makkah."
"6399","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Layth ibn Sad, from Musa ibn Ali, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to pray in three hours and to bury our dead in them: at sunrise until it becomes white and rises, at sunset until its setting is clear, and at midday in severe heat."
"6400","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, he said: We prayed over Aishah and Umm Salamah in the middle of al-Baqi among the graves. He said: And the imam on the day we prayed over Aishah was Abu Hurayrah, and Ibn Umar was present."
"6401","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When an obligatory prayer and a funeral coincide, the obligatory prayer is begun with."
"6402","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, he said: I saw al-Hasan when a funeral was placed at Maghrib prayer; he began and prayed over the funeral, then prayed Maghrib after that. I mentioned that to Qatadah, and he said: Had he begun with the obligatory prayer."
"6403","From Ma'mar, it reached me that Ali said: When the funeral and the obligatory prayer coincide, begin with the obligatory prayer. From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the same as the saying of Ali: the obligatory prayer is begun with."
"6404","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that a funeral was placed in the graveyard of Basrah when the sun turned yellow, and it was not prayed over until the sun set. Then Abu Barzah ordered the caller, who called, then he stood, and Abu Barzah stepped forward and led them in Maghrib prayer, and among the people was Anas ibn Malik, then he prayed over the funeral. And we adopt this."
"6405","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: My father saw people leaving the mosque to pray over a funeral, and said: What are these doing? Abu Bakr was prayed over only in the mosque."
"6406","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar was prayed over in the mosque."
"6407","From Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, from Aishah: that she ordered that the funeral of Sad ibn Malik be brought past her in the mosque when he died, so that she could supplicate for him. The people disapproved of that, so Aishah said: How quickly people forget; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Suhayl ibn al-Bayda only in the mosque."
"6408","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Salih ibn Nabhan, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral in the mosque, he has nothing."
"6409","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from a man he named called Muslim, from Kathir ibn Abbas, he said: I do not know but that he raised it (to the Prophet), he said: I would surely know that no funeral was ever prayed over in the mosque."
"6410","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, the foster-brother of Aishah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no man who dies, over whom a community of people prays and they seek forgiveness for him, except that they are granted intercession for him. He said: And the community is a hundred men. Al-Thawri and Ma'mar said it."
"6411","From al-Thawri, from Ismail, from a man, he said: A man came to Ali ibn Abi Talib and said: Will you not stand and pray over this funeral? He said: We are indeed standing, and nothing prays over him except his deed."
"6412","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a Christian woman pregnant by a Muslim dies while pregnant, she is buried with the people of her religion. Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: The people of her religion take charge of her, and she is buried with them."
"6413","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that an old man of the people of Sham informed him, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: that he buried a woman of the People of the Book, pregnant by a Muslim, in the graveyard of the Muslims."
"6414","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulayman ibn Musa: that Wathilah ibn al-Asqa buried a Christian woman who died while pregnant by a Muslim in a graveyard that was neither the graveyard of the Christians nor the graveyard of the Muslims, in between. Sulayman said: And the people of her religion take charge of her."
"6415","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is it binding upon people to straighten their rows over funerals as they straighten them in prayer? He said: No, they are only a people who say takbir and seek forgiveness."
"6416","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan: that when he prayed over a child, he would say: O Allah, make him for us a forerunner, and make him for us a reward."
"6417","From one who heard al-Hasan saying regarding prayer over a child: O Allah, make him a predecessor for his parents, a forerunner and a reward. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim, the same as the saying of al-Hasan."
"6418","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: When the newborn cries out at birth, the funeral prayer is performed over him. Al-Zuhri said: And he inherits when he cries out."
"6419","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He does not inherit until he cries out at birth, even if he moves. He said: Even sneezing would be with me like the birth-cry. Abd al-Razzaq said: And we adopt this."
"6420","Ma'mar informed us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed me, from Qatadah, he said: Even if the soul remained in him three (days), he would not inherit until he cries out at birth."
"6421","Ma'mar informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: I witnessed the midwives testifying regarding a boy who moved but did not cry out, and Shurayh did not grant him inheritance."
"6422","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When he cries out at birth, the funeral prayer is performed over him, and blood-money is due, and he inherits."
"6423","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan, he said: When he cries out at birth, the funeral prayer is performed over him."
"6424","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you pray over one who has cried out at birth and older? He said: Yes. I said: And a child born dead, three times? He said: I have not heard that prayer is performed over that."
"6425","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me regarding the miscarried fetus that is born, and the live infant: prayer is not performed over him until he cries out screaming."
"6426","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about the miscarried fetus that falls dead, is prayer performed over it? He said: No, until he cries; when he cries, prayer is performed over him and he inherits."
"6427","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar prayed over a small newborn, a miscarriage; I do not know whether he cried out or not. He prayed over him in his house, then sent him to be buried."
"6428","And one who saw Ibn Mujahid informed me: a miscarried fetus of his died, so he wrapped it in a cloth, placed it in his sleeve, went with it alone, buried it, and prayed over it."
"6429","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When his creation is complete and the soul is breathed into him, prayer is performed over him, even if he did not cry out. Qatadah said: And he is named, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection by his name, or he said: he is called by his name."
"6430","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Ziyad ibn Jubayr, from his father, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, he said: The miscarried fetus is prayed over, and supplication is made for his parents for well-being and mercy."
"6431","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When his creation is not complete, he is buried and no prayer is performed over him."
"6432","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Abu Bakr said: The most deserving of those we pray over are our children."
"6433","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the son of Mariyah the Copt, when he was sixteen months old."
"6434","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Sharik, from Bashir ibn Ghalib al-Asadi, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr said to Husayn ibn Ali: Upon whom is the ransom of the captive? He said: Upon the land for which we fight."
"6435","He said: And I asked him about the newborn, when does his share become due? He said: When he cries out at birth, his share becomes due."
"6436","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar used to assign a stipend for the boy when he cried out at birth."
"6437","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying regarding the newborn: he inherits when his voice is heard."
"6438","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: A young sister of mine died, so my father ordered a freedman of his regarding her, and he buried her; he did not go out for her, nor follow her. He said: I think he said: Nor did he pray over her."
"6439","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I saw Abu Hurayrah praying over a newborn who had never committed a sin at all, saying: O Allah, protect him from the punishment of the grave."
"6440","From al-Zuhri, he said: Prayer is performed over the child of fornication, because every newborn is born upon the fitrah. And al-Hasan said it."
"6441","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu al-Numan, from Amr ibn Yahya, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a child of fornication, and his mother had died in childbirth."
"6442","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, regarding the child of fornication: when he dies as a small child, no prayer is performed over him."
"6443","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about the child of fornication when he is born after he has cried out, is prayer performed over him? He said: Yes. I said: How, while he is such? He said: Because he was born upon the fitrah, the fitrah of Islam. I said: Then he grew up and became an evil man? He said: Prayer is performed over him. I said: And his mother died in childbirth? He said: I do not omit her, and he says: Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him. Ata said to me afterwards: Prayer is performed over the child of fornication when he cries out, and over his mother if she dies of childbirth, and over the two who invoke curses (lian), and over one subjected to retaliation, and over the stoned one, and over one who advances to fight then flees and is killed, and over one who dies an evil death. He said: I do not omit prayer over one who says: There is no god but Allah. He said: I said: Even after it has become clear to him that he is of the companions of Hell? He said: Who knows that these are of the companions of Hell? Ibn Jurayj said: And I asked Amr ibn Dinar, and he said the same as the saying of Ata."
"6444","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: They would not withhold prayer over anyone of the people of the qiblah."
"6445","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: No prayer is performed over the stoned one. Al-Zuhri said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the Aslami, and did not pray over him."
"6446","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned a woman, then prayed over her."
"6447","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri: Is prayer performed over one on whom retaliation is carried out in a hadd? He said: Yes, except one on whom retaliation is carried out by stoning."
"6448","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard Jabir ibn Samurah saying: A man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so has died. He said: He has not died. Then he came to him a second time, then a third. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How did he die? He said: He slaughtered himself with a barb he had. So he did not pray over him."
"6449","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: One who kills himself is prayed over, and the stoned one is prayed over."
"6450","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned two men; he prayed over one of them and did not pray over the other."
"6451","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from Ibrahim, he said: The Sunnah is that prayer is performed over the stoned one."
"6452","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Pray over one who says: There is no god but Allah, even if he is a very evil man. Say: O Allah, forgive the believing men and believing women, and the Muslim men and Muslim women. He said: And I do not know anyone of the people of knowledge who avoided prayer over one who says: There is no god but Allah."
"6453","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I have not known any of our companions to omit prayer over anyone of the people of the qiblah."
"6454","From Abu Mashar, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Maymun ibn Mihran: that he witnessed Ibn Umar praying over a child of fornication. It was said: Abu Hurayrah did not pray over him, and said: He is the worst of the three. So Ibn Umar said: He is the best of the three."
"6455","From al-Thawri, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Shabi, he said: When Ali stoned Sharahah al-Hamdaniyyah, her guardians came and said: What shall we do with her? He said to them: Do with her what you do with your dead, meaning washing her, praying over her, and the like."
"6456","Al-Thawri said, and Simak ibn Harb informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: I was with Ali when he stoned Shurahah. I said: She has died in her worst state. He said: So he struck me with a stick in his hand. I said: You have hurt me. He said: Even if I hurt you, she will never be punished after this, for Allah did not send down a hadd in the Quran which is then carried out upon its doer except that it is an expiation for him, like a debt for a debt."
"6457","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Hakam ibn Aban informed me that he heard Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, saying: Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Ubayy said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Let me kill my father, for he harms Allah and His Messenger. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not kill your father. Then he went, then returned to him and said: Let me kill him. He said: Do not kill your father. Then he came a third time and he said the same to him. He said: So perform ablution, O Messenger of Allah, that I may give him to drink, that his heart may soften. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution, and he gave him it to drink. He said: I have given you the ablution water of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to drink. He said: You have given me your mother's urine to drink. Ibn Abbas said: When his illness in which he died came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him, and they spoke words between them. Abd Allah said: I have understood what you say; bestow upon me, shroud me in this shirt of yours, and pray over me. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded him in that shirt of his and prayed over him. Ibn Abbas said: And Allah knows what prayer it was, and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never deceived any person."
"6458","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, that he heard Ikrimah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed the name of this son of Abd Allah; he named him Abd Allah, and his name had been al-Hubab."
"6459","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Abd Allah ibn Ubayy after he had been placed in his pit; he ordered regarding him, so he was brought out to him. He placed him upon his knees, clothed him in his shirt, and blew upon him of his saliva. And Allah knows best."
"6460","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah claiming that he heard at Madinah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode to Banu al-Harith and saw a funeral upon a wooden bier. He said: What is this? It was said: A slave of ours; he was an evil, displeasing, harsh slave. He said: Did this one pray? They said: Yes. He said: Did he say: Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Yes. He said: The angels almost came between me and him. Go back and perfect his washing, his shrouding, and his burial."
"6461","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Anbasah ibn Suhayl, from Muhammad ibn Zuhayr: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw at al-Baqi a black slave carrying a dead man. He said to those carrying him: What is this? They said: A slave of so-and-so. He said: What of him? They said: The most wicked of people, the most thieving, the most runaway, and the most embroiled in things of evil, mentioning things of him. He said: Bring me his master. He asked him about him, and he mentioned nearly what had been mentioned. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did he pray? They said: Yes. He said: And does he testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? They said: Yes. He said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the angels just now almost came between me and him. He called a blacksmith, who removed an iron, then he ordered regarding him; he was washed, then he shrouded him from himself, then prayed over him."
"6462","From Ma'mar, from a man, he said: I asked al-Shabi about prayer over captives, and he said: Pray over whoever of them prayed. Ma'mar said: And when prayer is performed over the captive, prayer is performed over his child."
"6463","From al-Thawri, he said: When the child, of the captives or others, is between his parents and they are both polytheists, no prayer is performed over him. If he is not between his parents, then he is a Muslim; when he dies as a child, prayer is performed over him. He said: And Hammad said: When you own the child, he is a Muslim."
"6464","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Abi Suayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: When the day of Uhud came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked upon the martyrs killed that day and said: I have borne witness over these, so wrap them in their blood. He would bury two men and three in one grave, and ask: Which of them knew most Quran, and they would put him forward. Jabir said: My father and my uncle were buried in one grave that day."
"6465","From Ma'mar, he said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, he saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the martyrs on the day of Uhud: These have passed on, and I have borne witness over them, and they did not consume anything of their rewards. But you consume of your rewards, and I do not know what you will innovate after me."
"6466","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: They did not pray over the martyrs on the day of Uhud."
"6467","From al-Thawri, from al-Shaybani, from Abu Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the slain of Uhud."
"6468","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the slain of Badr."
"6469","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I did not see them washing the martyr, nor embalming him, nor is he shrouded. I said: How do we pray over him? He said: As is prayed over the other who is not a martyr."
"6470","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Muawiyah ordered the killing of Hujr ibn Adi al-Kindi. Hujr said: Do not release a fetter from me, or he said: iron, and shroud me in my clothes and my blood."
"6471","From al-Thawri, from Mukhawwal, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, from Zayd ibn Suhan, he said: Do not wash blood from me, nor remove a garment except the two leather socks. Bury me in the earth a burial, for I am a disputing man; I shall dispute on the Day of Resurrection."
"6472","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Musab, from a man, from the son of Zayd, he said: Bury us, and what the dust touches of our blood. He said: And Ammar al-Duhni informed me, he said: Zayd said: Tighten my clothes upon me, and bury me and my maternal brother in one grave, meaning his brother Sarhan, for we are a disputing people."
"6473","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Sad ibn Ubayd, who was called in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the Reciter. He had met an enemy and fled from them. Umar said to him: Would you go to Sham? That Allah may bestow upon you. He said: No, except to those from whom I fled. He said: So he addressed them at al-Qadisiyyah and said: Tomorrow, Allah willing, we shall meet the enemy, and we shall be martyred. So do not wash blood from us, and we shall be shrouded only in a garment that was upon us."
"6474","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: We asked Sulayman ibn Musa: How is prayer over the martyr according to them? He said: Like its form over others. He said: And we asked about the burial of the martyr, and he said: As for when he is on the battlefield, we bury him as he is; we neither wash him, nor shroud him, nor embalm him. But when we bring him back with a spark of life in him, we wash him, shroud him, and embalm him. We found people upon that, and those who passed before us of the people were upon it."
"6475","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd, he said: When the martyr dies on the battlefield, he is buried as he is. If he dies after being carried back, he is treated as the other is treated."
"6476","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Umar was the best of martyrs; he was washed, prayed over, and shrouded, because he lived after his stabbing."
"6477","al-Hasan ibn Umarah informed us, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, he said: Ali was washed, shrouded, and prayed over."
"6478","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, he said: When the martyr dies on his spot, he is not washed; when he is carried alive, he is washed."
"6479","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Isa, from al-Shabi, he said: He was asked about a man killed by bandits, and said: He is not washed."
"6480","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard Ikrimah informed me, he saying: Prayer is performed over the martyr, and he is not washed, for Allah has made him pure."
"6481","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyib, they said: The martyr is washed, for every dead person is in a state of major impurity."
"6482","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me, from Ibn Abi Ammar, from Shaddad ibn al-Had: that a Bedouin man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), believed in him, followed him, and said: I shall emigrate with you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted him to some of his Companions. When the expedition of Khaybar came, or he said: Hunayn, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gained spoils to be divided, and he apportioned for him and gave his companions what he had apportioned; and he was pasturing their riding animals. When he came, they handed it to him. He said: What is this? They said: A share the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apportioned for you. He took it and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What is this, O Muhammad? He said: A share I apportioned for you. He said: I did not follow you for this, but I followed you that I be shot here, and he pointed with his hand to his throat with an arrow, then die, then enter Paradise. He said: If you are truthful with Allah, He will be truthful with you. They stayed a little, then rose to fight the enemy. He was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried, an arrow having struck him where he had pointed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is it he? Is it he? They said: Yes. He said: He was truthful with Allah, so He was truthful with him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded him in the cloak of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then put him forward and prayed over him. Among what appeared of his prayer was: O Allah, this is Your servant; he went out emigrating in Your path, killed as a martyr."
"6483","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man asked Ata: Is prayer performed over the martyr? He said: Yes. It was said to him: While he is in Paradise? He said: Prayer was performed over the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Jurayj said: It reached me that the martyrs of Badr were buried as they were."
"6484","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from al-Shabi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Hamzah on the day of Uhud seventy prayers; whenever a man was brought, he prayed over him, while Hamzah lay placed, prayed over along with him."
"6485","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Every skin is removed from the martyr, meaning when he is killed."
"6486","From Israil and others, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: The slain one's leather socks, trousers, and cap are removed, or he said: his turban. A garment is added or a garment is reduced until it is odd."
"6487","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: When Muawiyah wanted to run the water channel, he said: Whoever has a slain one, let him come to his slain one, meaning the slain of Uhud. He said: So they brought them out supple, bending. He said: A pick struck the foot of one of them and it burst forth blood. Abu Said said: No denier shall deny after this, ever."
"6488","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: One of the family of Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah saw in sleep that he saw him, and he said: You have buried me in a place where water has reached me; move me from it. So they moved him and brought him out like a silqah (unchanged), nothing of him changed except some hairs of his beard."
"6489","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad, from Nubayh, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We had carried the slain on the day of Uhud to bury them, and the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and said: Bury the slain in their places of falling. So we returned them."
"6490","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla informed me, he said: The martyr is not buried in footwear of leather socks, nor sandals, nor weapons, nor a ring. He said: We bury him in the waist-wrap and clothes. He said: And it reached me from Ibrahim al-Nakhai: He is not buried with a patch."
"6491","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: A woman who died at al-Bayda was mentioned to Umar; people would pass by her and not bury her, until Kulayb passed by her and buried her. Umar said: I hope good for Kulayb because of her. He said: Umar asked Abd Allah ibn Umar about her, and he said: I did not see her. He said: Had you seen her and not buried her, I would have made you an example. Ma'mar said: And I heard al-Zuhri saying: Abu Luluah stabbed twelve men; six of them died, among them Umar and Kulayb, and six of them lived; then he slaughtered himself with a dagger."
"6492","From Ma'mar, it reached me from Abu Hurayrah: that when a funeral was passed by him, he would say: Go forth, for we are going in the morning, or: go in the morning, for we are going in the evening. An eloquent admonition, and a swift heedlessness; the first goes and the last remains, having no sense."
"6493","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, he said: It is said when a funeral is seen: Allah is Greatest; this is what Allah and His Messenger promised, and Allah and His Messenger spoke truth. O Allah, increase us in faith and submission. We are surrendered to Allah, our Lord. And we adopt this, meaning: surrender is submission, i.e., we are Yours."
"6494","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Abu Hurayrah, when asked about the funeral, would say: It is you; if you refuse, then it is I."
"6495","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Three are of the deeds of Jahiliyyah: wailing, food over the dead, and a woman unrelated to them spending the night with the family of the dead. Al-Thawri mentioned it, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, from Abu al-Bakhtari."
"6496","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Jafar ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi narrated to me, from his father Khalid, from Abd Allah ibn Jafar, he said: When the news of Jafar's death came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Prepare food for the family of Jafar, for there has come to them what occupies them."
"6497","From a man of the people of Madinah, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his mother Asma bint Umays, she said: When Jafar was stricken, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: O Asma, do not say foul words, nor strike the chest. She said: And Fatimah came, while he was saying: O son of my uncle. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let the weeping woman weep over the like of Jafar. She said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to his family and said: Prepare food for the family of Jafar, for they are occupied today. He said: And Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr informed me, from Sawdah bint Harithah, wife of Amr ibn Hazm, she said: It used to be commanded that we prepare food for the family of the dead."
"6498","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I heard al-Hasan saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Patience is at the first shock, and tears man cannot control; the outpouring of a man toward his brother."
"6499","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Kathir, he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a woman afflicted with the loss of her child; he heard from her what he disliked, and stood over her admonishing her. She said to him: Go away from me, for there is not in your breast what is in my breast. He turned from her, and it was said to her: Woe to you, do you not know who stood over you? He is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So she followed him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not know you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go away from you; patience is only at the first shock."
"6500","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: It reached me that patience is at the first shock."
"6501","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent: that my daughter is dying, so come to us. He sent back: He conveys peace to you, and says: To Allah belongs what He took, and to Him belongs what He gave, and everything with Him is for a named term. So let her be patient and seek reward. She sent back swearing that he must come. He said: So he stood, and we stood, and with him were Muadh ibn Jabal, Ubayy ibn Kab, and Sad ibn Ubadah. He said: He took the little girl while her soul rattled in her chest, and his eyes shed tears. Sad said: O Messenger of Allah, what is this? He said: This is mercy Allah placed in the hearts of His servants; Allah only shows mercy to the merciful among His servants."
"6502","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed me of it, from Ibn Abi Husayn, from Makhul, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while leaning upon Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, while Ibrahim, son of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was breathing his last. When he saw him, his eyes shed tears. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: O Messenger of Allah, do you weep? When the Muslims see you weep, they weep. He said: When his tear welled up, he said: This is only kinship-mercy, and whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy. I only forbid people from wailing, and from eulogizing the dead with what is not in him. When he passed away, he said: Were it not that it is a binding promise encompassing all, a trodden path, and that the last of us joins the first, we would have found other than what we found. And indeed we are grieved for you, O Ibrahim. The eye sheds tears, and the heart feels, and we do not say what angers the Lord. And the remainder of his nursing is in Paradise."
"6503","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik: that Fatimah wept over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O my father, how near to his Lord; O my father, to Jibril do we announce him; O my father, the Garden of Firdaws is his abode."
"6504","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Wahb ibn Kaysan also, from Muhammad ibn Amr: that Salamah ibn al-Azraq informed him that he was sitting with Ibn Umar one day in the market, and a funeral passed by, wept over. Ibn Umar disapproved of that and rebuked them. Salamah ibn al-Azraq said to him: Do not say that, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, for I bear witness over Abu Hurayrah; I heard him saying: A woman of the kin of Marwan died, and I attended her. Marwan ordered the weeping women to be beaten. Abu Hurayrah said: Leave them, O Abu Abd al-Malik, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a funeral wept over, and I was with him, and with him was Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar rebuked those weeping, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Leave them, O Ibn al-Khattab, for the soul is afflicted, the eye sheds tears, and the covenant is recent. He said: Did you hear it, he said? I said: Yes. He said: Allah and His Messenger know best."
"6505","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, he said: A daughter of Uthman ibn Affan died at Makkah, and we came to witness her, or he said: we came to attend her. Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas attended her. He said: I was sitting between them; I sat beside one of them, then the other came and sat beside me. Abd Allah ibn Umar said to Amr ibn Uthman, facing him: Will you not forbid weeping, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dead is punished by the weeping of his family over him. Ibn Abbas said: Umar used to say something of that. Then he narrated, he said: I set out with Umar from Makkah until, when we were at al-Bayda, he saw riders under the shade of a tree. He said: Go and see who these riders are. I looked, and it was Suhayb. I informed him. He said: Call him to me. He said: I returned to Suhayb and said: Depart and join the Commander of the Faithful. When Umar was stricken, Suhayb entered weeping, saying: O my brother, O my companion. Umar said: O Suhayb, do you weep over me, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dead is punished by the weeping of his family. Ibn Abbas said: When Umar died, I mentioned that to Aishah. She said: May Allah have mercy on Umar. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not narrate that Allah punishes the believers by anyone's weeping, but he said: Allah increases the disbeliever's punishment by his family's weeping over him. He said: And Aishah said: The Quran suffices you: No bearer of burdens bears the burden of another. He said: Ibn Abbas said at that: By Allah, He makes laugh and weep. Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: By Allah, Ibn Umar said nothing of it."
"6506","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: that Suhayb said to Umar: O my brother, O my companion. Umar said: Be silent, woe to you; did you not hear us narrating that one wailed over is punished? From Jafar, from Thabit, from Abu Rafi, that he heard that from Umar, like the hadith of Ma'mar."
"6507","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard an old man called Abu Umar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying while at the funeral of Rafi ibn Khadij, when women stood weeping over Rafi, and he seated them repeatedly, then said to them: Woe to you, Rafi ibn Khadij is an old man with no strength for punishment, and the dead is punished by his family's weeping over him."
"6508","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Aishah said: May Allah have mercy on Umar and Ibn Umar; they heard something they did not retain. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only passed by a perishing one whose family wept over him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: His family weep over him, while he is being punished. She said: And the man had entered ihram."
"6509","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When Abu Bakr died, he was wept over. Umar said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dead is punished by the weeping of the living. They refused but to weep. Umar said to Hisham ibn al-Walid: Stand and bring out the women. Aishah said: I shall bring you out. Umar said: Enter, for I have permitted you. He said: So he entered, and Aishah said: Are you bringing me out, O my son? He said: As for you, I have permitted. He said: He kept bringing them out at him, woman by woman, striking them with the stick, until he brought out Umm Farwah, separating them, or he said: separating the lamenters."
"6510","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: When Khalid ibn al-Walid died, women gathered in the house of Maymunah weeping. Umar came, with him Ibn Abbas, and with him the stick. He said: O Abu Abd Allah, enter upon the Mother of the Believers, order her to veil herself, and bring them out to me. He said: He kept bringing them out to him, while he struck them with the stick. The veil of one of the women fell, and they said: O Commander of the Faithful, her veil. He said: Leave her; she has no sanctity. Ma'mar used to marvel at his saying: She has no sanctity."
"6511","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abd al-Karim, he said: Nasr ibn Asim narrated to me: that Umar ibn al-Khattab heard wailing at Madinah at night; he went to it, entered, dispersed the women, caught the wailing woman and struck her with the stick. Her veil fell, and they said: Her hair, O Commander of the Faithful. He said: Yes, she has no sanctity."
"6512","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Aishah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who tears garments, strikes cheeks, and calls with the call of Jahiliyyah."
"6513","From Ma'mar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: We entered upon al-Ashari, and his concubine wept over him. We forbade her and said: Do you weep over the like of Abu Musa? He said: Leave her; let her shed a bucket or two of her tears. But I call you to witness that I am free of whoever shaves, or tears, or rends."
"6514","From Ma'mar, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said to Umar: Women of Banu al-Mughirah have gathered in the house of Khalid ibn al-Walid weeping over him, and we dislike to trouble you; would that you forbade them. He said: It is no harm upon them to shed a bucket or two of their tears over Abu Sulayman, so long as there is no dust-throwing or shrieking, meaning screaming."
"6515","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibn Muanik, or from Abu Muanik, from Abu Malik al-Ashari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Four remain of the affair of Jahiliyyah: boasting of lineages, disparaging ancestries, seeking rain by the stars, and wailing. And the wailing woman, when she dies without repenting, will be clothed in garments of tar and a coat of the flame of Fire."
"6516","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Three people never abandon: disparaging lineages, seeking rain by the stars, and wailing."
"6517","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Three are of the affair of Jahiliyyah: wailing, food over the dead, and a woman unrelated to them spending the night with the family of the dead."
"6518","From al-Thawri, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: Food over the dead is of the affair of Jahiliyyah, a woman spending the night with the family of the dead is of the affair of Jahiliyyah, and wailing is of the affair of Jahiliyyah."
"6519","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took pledge from women when they pledged: that they not wail. They said: O Messenger of Allah, women aided us in Jahiliyyah; shall we aid them in Islam? He said: There is no aiding in Islam, nor shighar in Islam, nor slaughter at graves in Islam, nor j engaging nor junub-avoidance (in racing spoils). Whoever plunders is not of us."
"6520","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took pledge from women when they pledged to him: that they not wail, nor go out for men's talk. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: We are absent and we have guests. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not mean those."
"6521","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that Hafsah sought permission to enter upon her father. He said to those with him: Stand. She entered, and while she was with him, he fainted. She wept, and he said: Did you know, or did you not hear, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dead is punished by the weeping of the living."
"6522","From Ma'mar, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: My father was killed on the day of Uhud; he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and placed before him mutilated, disfigured. He said: I bent over him weeping, and the people consoled me, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me and did not forbid me, until he was lifted. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The angels did not cease around him until he was lifted. He said: My father had a debt, and creditors would come to the date-palms, look at them, and deem them little. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: When you wish to harvest, inform me. He said: I came to him, and he went with me until he stood among them and prayed for blessing. He said: So I paid what was upon my father, and much food remained for us."
"6523","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Uhud, he heard lamentation and weeping of the people of Madinah. He said: What is this? It was said: The Ansar weep over their slain. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But Hamzah has no weeping women. That reached the Ansar, so they gathered their women and brought them into the house of Hamzah weeping over him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard them and said: What is this? It was said: When the Ansar heard you saying: But Hamzah has no weeping women, they gathered their women weeping over him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Good, and forbade them from wailing."
"6524","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed of a report raised to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Abd Allah ibn Thabit, Abu al-Rabi, visiting him in his illness twice; he died when he came to him the last of the two. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out to him once or twice, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have been separated from Abu al-Rabi; indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return. When his daughters and his brother's daughters heard that, they stood weeping. Jabr ibn Atik said to them: Do not harm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them, O Abu Abd Allah; let them weep over Abu al-Rabi while he is among them; when the soul departs, let them not weep over him. His daughter said: I had prepared your outfit for the path of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The reward of Abu al-Rabi has fallen upon his intention. What do you count as martyrdom? They said: Being killed in the path of Allah. He said: Then the martyrs of my community are few. They said: Who are the martyrs, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The plague-stricken is a martyr, the one with abdominal illness is a martyr, the one burnt is a martyr, the drowned is a martyr, the one with pleurisy is a martyr, the one with grief is a martyr, and the woman who dies in childbirth is a martyr. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded him in his shirt."
"6525","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from a man, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: You are the Messenger of Allah, and revelation descends upon you, and my two sons have been stricken where you know. If they are believers, we shall say of them what we know; if they are hypocrites, we shall neither weep over them nor gladden the eye with them. He said: Rather they are believers, and they are of the people of Paradise. She said: Now then I shall weep profusely over them."
"6526","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Shabi, he said: Ibn Rawahah lost consciousness, and his wife began saying, alas for this and alas for that, and when he recovered he said: There was nothing you said but that it was said to me, are you really so? And I would say, no."
"6527","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail, from Qays, from Ibn Masud, he said: When Zayd ibn Harithah was killed, Usamah stayed away from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and did not come to him, then he came to him after that and stood before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his eyes filled with tears, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept, and when his tears flowed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you stay away from us, then come to grieve us? He said: The next day he came to him, and when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him coming he said: I am bound to meet from you today what I met from you yesterday, and when he drew near his eye filled with tears, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept."
"6528","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that Abu Bakr was seized by the stupor of death, and she wept over him, meaning Aishah, with a verse of poetry: Whoever never ceases with his tears held back must one day shed them. He said: Then he recovered and said: Rather, the stupor of death has come with the truth, that is what you used to turn away from."
"6529","From al-Thawri, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit al-Qurashi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is afflicted by a calamity, let him remember his calamity by me, so that it consoles him for his calamity. He said: He said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), are there horses in Paradise? For I love horses. He said: Allah will admit you into Paradise, Allah willing, and you will not wish to ride a horse of red ruby that flies with you wherever you wish in Paradise but that it will be done."
"6530","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: Umar ibn Abi Salamah informed me, from his mother Umm Salamah, from her husband Abu Salamah, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no one among the Muslims who is afflicted by a calamity and says, we belong to Allah and to Him we return, O Allah, I seek the reward of my calamity with You, O Allah, replace it for me with better than it. I began saying to myself, who is better than Abu Salamah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and proposed to me, and I married him."
"6531","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, and he said: The first drop that drips of the blood of the martyr, he is forgiven thereby for what preceded of his sin, then Allah sends to him two angels with sweet basil of Paradise and a striped garment, and at the outskirts of the heaven are angels saying, glory be to Allah, there has come today from the earth a pleasant scent, a pleasant breeze, and it does not pass by a gate but that it is opened for it, nor by an angel but that he blesses it and accompanies it until it is brought to the Most Merciful, and it prostrates to Him before the angels, and the angels prostrate after it, then he is ordered to join the martyrs, and he finds them in green meadows and garments of silk, near a bull and a whale that are fed each day a feeding unlike that of yesterday, and the whale stays in the rivers of Paradise, and when evening comes the bull gores it with its horn and slaughters it for them, and they eat of its flesh and find in its flesh the taste of every fragrance of the rivers of Paradise, and the bull remains grazing in Paradise, and in the morning it goes to it, then the whale, and it strikes it with its tail and slaughters it for them, and they eat of its flesh and find in its flesh the taste of every fruit of the fruits of Paradise, and they look at their dwellings morning and evening, asking Allah that the Hour rise, and when the believer dies Allah sends to him two angels with sweet basil of Paradise and a cloth of Paradise in which his soul is taken, and it is said: Come out, O good soul, to rest and sweet basil, and your Lord is not angry with you, and it comes out like the sweetest scent anyone ever smelled with his nose, and at the outskirts of the heaven are angels saying, glory be to Allah, there has come today from the earth a pleasant scent and a noble breeze, and it does not pass by a gate but that it is opened for it, nor by an angel but that he blesses it and accompanies it until it is brought to the Most Merciful, and the angels prostrate before it and it prostrates after them, then Mikail is called and it is said: Take this soul and place it with the souls of the believers until I ask you about them on the Day of Resurrection, and he is ordered to his grave, and it is widened for him seventy in length and seventy in width, and sweet basil is scattered for him in it, and it is covered with silk, and if he had something of the Quran its light clothes him, and if he had nothing a light like the sun is made for him, and his likeness is that of the bride whom none wakes but the dearest of his family to him, and when the disbeliever dies Allah sends to him two angels with a cloth of coarse hair, fouler than every foul thing and rougher than every rough thing, and it is said: Come out, O wicked soul, and evil is what you sent ahead for yourself, and it comes out like the foulest scent anyone ever smelled with his nose, then he is ordered to his grave and it is narrowed over him until his ribs differ, then snakes are sent over him as though their necks were those of Bactrian camels eating his flesh, and deaf, dumb and blind angels are set over him who do not hear his voice, nor see him to have mercy on him, nor tire when they strike, asking Allah to make that lasting over him until he ends in the Fire."
"6532","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When the dead one is placed in his grave, the prayer is at his head, and the zakat at his right, and the fast at his left, and charity and kinship and good and kindness to people at his feet, and he is approached from before his head, and the prayer says, there is no entry from before me, then he is approached from before his right, and the zakat says, there is no entry from before me, then he is approached from before his left, and the fast says, there is no entry from before me, then he is approached from before his feet, and the charity says, there is no entry from before me. He said: Then he is sat up. Abu Hurayrah said: He hears the striking of their sandals. He said: Then he is sat up, and the sun is pictured for him as having drawn near to setting, and he says, leave me to pray, and it is said to him, you shall do so, and it is said to him, what do you say about this man? He says, is it Muhammad? They say, yes. He says, I bear witness that he came with the truth from Allah. He said: Then it is said to him, upon it you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you shall be raised, Allah willing. He said: And that is His saying, Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter. He said: Then a door of the Fire is opened for him, and he looks at his dwellings in it, and it is said to him, had you disobeyed these would have been your dwellings, and he increases in bliss and joy, and his grave is widened for him, he said, seventy. Abd al-Rahman ibn Yahya ibn Hantab said: Then it is said, sleep the sleep of the bride, whom none wakes but the dearest of creation to him. The account returns to Abu Hurayrah, he said: His soul is placed in the pleasant breeze in the bellies of birds clinging among trees of the trees of Paradise, or clinging to the trees of Paradise. He said: And the bodies return to what they were created for. He said: And the disbeliever is approached from before his head, and nothing is found for him, and he is sat up, then it is said to him, what did you say about this man? Twice, he does not remember him until he is prompted, and he says, Muhammad. He said: I used to say what the people say. And it is said to him, you spoke truly, upon it you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you shall be raised, Allah willing. Then a door of Paradise is opened, and he sees its dwellings, and it is said to him, had you acted and obeyed Allah these would have been your dwellings, and he increases in regret and ruin. He said: Then it is closed over him, and a door of the Fire is opened for him, and he sees his dwellings in it and what Allah has prepared for him of punishment, and he increases in regret and ruin, and his grave is narrowed over him until his ribs meet, and that is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: A straitened living. He said: And his soul is placed in Sijjin."
"6533","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cursed are the women who visit graves."
"6534","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever visits graves is not of us."
"6535","From al-Thawri, from al-Mujalid ibn Said, he said: I heard al-Shabi say: Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not forbidden visiting graves, I would have visited the grave of my daughter."
"6536","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike visiting graves."
"6537","From Mamar, he said: Ata al-Khurasani informed us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them, for they remind of the Hereafter, and I had forbidden you from the nabidh of jars, so make nabidh in every vessel, but avoid every intoxicant, and I had forbidden you from eating the meat of sacrifices after three days, so eat and take provision and store."
"6538","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to pass by the grave of his brother Waqid, and would stand over it and supplicate for him and pray over him. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the same."
"6539","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come to your dead and greet them and pray over them, for you have a lesson in them."
"6540","Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: And I saw Aishah visiting the grave of her brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and he died at al-Hubshi and was buried in Makkah."
"6541","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Qays ibn Makhramah informed us, he said: I heard Aishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: Shall I not tell you about me and about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? We said: Yes. She said: When it was my night, he turned and placed his sandals by his feet, and placed his cloak until he spread the edge of his waist-wrap over his bed, and he stayed only as long as he thought I had slept, then he put on his sandals quietly and took his cloak quietly, and I put my shift over my head and covered my head, then I veiled myself with my waist-wrap, and I went out after him until he came to al-Baqi, and he raised his hand three times and stood long, then he turned and I turned, and he hastened and I hastened, and he jogged and I jogged, and he ran and I ran, and I outpaced him and entered, and no sooner had I lain down than he entered, and he said: What is with you, O Aishah, panting with raised chest? I said: Nothing. He said: Will you tell me, or will the Subtle, the Aware tell me? I said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you, and I told him the story. He said: Are you the black shape I saw before me? I said: Yes. She said: Then he poked my chest with a poke that hurt me, then he said: Did you think Allah and His Messenger would wrong you? And I said: Whatever people conceal, Allah knows, yes. He said: Jibril came to me when you saw, and he would not enter upon you while you had laid off your clothes, so he called me and hid from you, and I answered him and hid it from you, and I thought you had slept, and I disliked waking you, and I feared you would be lonely, so he ordered me to come to the people of al-Baqi and seek forgiveness for them. She said: I said: What shall I say? He said: Say: Peace be upon the people of the abodes among the believers and the Muslims, may Allah have mercy on those of us who went before and those who come after, and we, Allah willing, shall join you."
"6542","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, he said: Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to visit the grave of Hamzah every Friday."
"6543","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda, from Ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day, and we went out with him until we reached the graveyards, and he ordered us and we sat, then we stepped over the graves until we reached one grave among them, and he sat by it and conversed with it long, then the weeping of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose as he wept, and we wept for his weeping, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came forward, and Umar ibn al-Khattab met him and said: What made you weep, O Messenger of Allah? He said: It has made us weep and frightened us. And he took the hand of Umar, then gestured to us and we came to him, and he said: Did my weeping frighten you? And we said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The grave you saw me at is the grave of my mother Aminah bint Wahb, and I sought permission of my Lord to visit her and He permitted me, then I sought His permission to seek forgiveness for her and He did not permit me, and there was sent down: It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, the verse, and: The seeking of forgiveness of Ibrahim for his father. And there seized me what seizes the child for the parent of tenderness, and that is what made me weep. Behold, I had forbidden you three things: visiting graves, and eating the meat of sacrifices beyond three days so that it suffices you, and the nabidh of vessels, so visit them, for they make one renounce this world and remind of the Hereafter, and eat the meat of sacrifices and spend of it as you wish, for I only forbade you when good was little, and as relief for the people. Behold, the vessel does not forbid anything, every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6544","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Safwan narrated to me that Aminah bint Wahb, the mother of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was buried in the ravine of Abu Dabb."
"6545","From a man of the people of Madinah, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to the graves of the martyrs at the turn of the year and say: Peace be upon you for what you endured, and excellent is the final home. He said: And Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman used to do that."
"6546","From al-Bajali, from al-Kalbi, from al-Asbagh ibn Nubatah, that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to come to the grave of Hamzah, and she had placed a marker over it so she would know it. And it was mentioned that the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and of Abu Bakr and Umar had small pebbles over them, meaning small stones."
"6547","Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid, he said: The greeting over graves is: Peace be upon the Muslims and Muslim women, and the believing men and believing women, of the people of the abodes, and may Allah have mercy on those of us who went before, and we, Allah willing, shall join you. Mamar said: Qatadah used to mention something like this and add: You are our forerunners and we are your followers, and we, Allah willing, shall join you."
"6548","Malik informed us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yaqub, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a graveyard, or he said: by al-Baqi, then he said: Peace be upon the people of the abodes therein among the Muslims, the abode of a people who died, and we are following in their tracks, or he said: in your tracks we shall join."
"6549","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to go out with groups of his Companions to the burial ground of Baqi al-Gharqad and say: Peace be upon you, O people of the graves, if only you knew from what Allah has saved you of what will come after you. Then he would turn to his Companions, among whom that day were the distinguished ones, and say: Are you better, or are they? And they would say: We hope they are not better than us, we emigrated as they emigrated and we strove as they strove. And he would say: Rather they are better than you, they have passed on and did not consume any of their rewards, while you consume from your rewards, for they have passed on and I have testified for them, and I do not know what you will innovate after me."
"6550","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, that Ibn Umar would not pass by a grave without greeting it."
"6551","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Qays ibn Makhramah, from Aishah, she said: I had asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How do we say in greeting the graves? And he said: Say: Peace be upon the people of the abodes among the believers and the Muslims, and may Allah have mercy on those of us who went before and those who come after, and we, Allah willing, shall join you."
"6552","Yahya ibn al-Ala informed us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: Abu Hurayrah and a companion of his passed by a grave, and Abu Hurayrah said: Greet. The man said: Do I greet the grave? And Abu Hurayrah said: If he ever saw you in this world even one day, he knows you now."
"6553","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: When Ibn Umar returned from a journey, he would come to the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, peace be upon you, O Abu Bakr, peace be upon you, O father. And Abdullah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar. Mamar said: I mentioned that to Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, and he said: We know none of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who did that, except Ibn Umar."
"6554","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Miqdam, that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib, and he saw people greeting the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: No prophet remained on the earth more than forty days."
"6555","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, from a man called Suhayl, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hasan ibn Ali, he said: He saw people at the grave and forbade them, and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not take my grave as a festival, and do not take your houses as graves, and send blessings upon me wherever you are, for your blessings reach me."
"6556","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Anas ibn Malik, narrating that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I passed by Musa on the night I was taken by night, while he was standing praying in his grave."
"6557","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, from Nafi ibn Sarjis, that Sad ibn Abi Waqqas fell ill at Makkah behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to al-Taif, and when he returned, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Amr al-Qari: If he dies, then here, and he pointed to the road of Madinah."
"6558","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sad narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left a man in charge over Sad ibn Abi Waqqas while he was at Makkah, and said: If he dies, do not bury him until you take him out of it."
"6559","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Khuthaym from Nafi ibn Sarjis, he said: We visited Abu Waqid al-Bakri in his illness in which he died, and he died and was buried in the graves of the Muhajirun. He said: And people of the Ansar among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died and were buried there in the graves of the Muhajirun. He said: And I followed some of them. It reached me that they are the graves below Fakhkh, and I kept hearing, while a boy: They are the graves of the Muhajirun."
"6560","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Sayfi, he said: Whoever dies and is buried in that graveyard will be raised secure on the Day of Resurrection. He said: And I used to hear before that, that whoever dies in the Haram, that is for him."
"6561","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sad informed me, from al-Araj: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered al-Saib ibn Abd al-Qari and said: If Sad dies, do not bury him in Makkah."
"6562","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar instructed them: Do not bury him, but Abdullah ibn Khalid overcame them until they buried him in the Haram."
"6563","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Khidash informed me, that Ibn Abbas said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) overlooked the graveyard while on its first road, he pointed with his hand beyond al-Sufrah and said: What a fine graveyard. I said to the one informing me: Did he single out the ravine? He said: Thus we used to hear: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled out the ravine facing the House."
"6564","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, he said: I do not like to be buried in al-Baqi; to be buried elsewhere is dearer to me than to be buried in it; a man is but one of two: either a wrongdoer, and I do not like to be with him in his grave, or righteous, and I do not like that his bones be moved."
"6565","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Judan, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When people are gathered on the Day of Resurrection, I shall be raised among the people of al-Baqi."
"6566","From Mamar, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from al-Minhal ibn Umar, from Zadham, from al-Bara, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to a funeral, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat at the grave, and we sat around him as though birds were upon our heads, while a niche was being dug for him, and he said: I seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave, three times. Then he said: When the believer is in the approach of the Hereafter and the severance from this world, angels descend upon him, as though their faces were the sun, with each one a shroud and embalming perfume, and they sit from him as far as the eye can see, until when his soul comes out every angel between the heaven and the earth blesses him, and every angel in the heaven, and the gates of heaven are opened for him, and there is no people of a gate but that they call upon Allah that his soul be taken up before them, and when his soul is taken up before them, they say: O Lord, Your servant so-and-so. And He says: Return him, for I covenanted to them that from it I created them, and into it I shall return them, and from it I shall bring them out another time. And he hears the striking of the sandals of his companions when they turn away from him, and a comer comes to him and says: Who is your Lord? What is your religion? Who is your prophet? And he says: My Lord is Allah, and my religion is Islam, and my prophet is Muhammad, upon him be peace. And he rebukes him and says: Who is your Lord? What is your religion? Who is your prophet? And it is the last trial presented to the believer, and that is when He says: Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter. And he says: My Lord is Allah, and my religion is Islam, and my prophet is Muhammad, upon him be peace. And he says to him: You spoke truly. Then a comer comes to him, handsome of face, sweet of scent, handsome of clothes, and says to him: Rejoice in honour from Allah and lasting bliss. And he says: May Allah gladden you with good, who are you? And he says: I am your righteous deed; by Allah, you were swift in obedience to Allah and slow in disobedience to Allah, so may Allah reward you with good. Then a door of Paradise and a door of the Fire are opened for him, and it is said: This is your dwelling had you disobeyed Allah; Allah has settled you with this instead. And when he sees what is in Paradise, he says: My Lord, hasten the rising of the Hour, so that I may return to my family and wealth. And it is said: Be still. And when the disbeliever is in severance from this world and approach of the Hereafter, stern harsh angels descend to him and tear out his soul, as the great many-pronged skewer is torn from wet wool, and his breath is torn out with the veins, and when his soul comes out every angel between the heaven and the earth curses him, and every angel in the heaven, and the gates of heaven are locked, and there is no people of a gate but that they call that his soul not be taken up before them, and when his soul is taken up, they say: Our Lord, this is Your servant so-and-so. And He says: Return him; I covenanted to them that from it I created them, and into it I shall return them, and from it I shall bring them out another time. He said: And he hears the striking of the sandals of his companions when they turn away from him, and a comer comes to him and says: Who is your Lord? And what is your religion? And who is your prophet? And he says: My Lord is Allah, and my religion is Islam, and my prophet is Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And he rebukes him with a severe rebuke and says: Who is your Lord? And what is your religion? And who is your prophet? And he says: I do not know. And he says: You did not know, nor did you recite. And a comer comes to him, foul of clothes, stinking of scent, and says: Rejoice in disgrace from Allah and lasting punishment. And he says: And you, may Allah gladden you with evil, who are you? And he says: I am your wicked deed; you were slow to the obedience of Allah and swift in disobedience to Allah, so may Allah reward you with evil. Then a blind, deaf, dumb one is set over him, in his hand a mallet that had he struck a mountain with it, it would have been dust, and he strikes him a blow and he becomes dust, then Allah restores him as he was, and he strikes him another blow, and he shrieks a shriek heard by everything except the two weighty ones, then a door of the Fire is opened for him, and a bed of the Fire is spread for him. Mamar said: And I heard it from Muadh, that he said: Everything hears it except the two weighty ones."
"6567","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Dinar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar: How will you be, O Umar, with the two testers of the grave, when they come to you digging with their fangs and treading upon their hair, their eyes like flashing lightning and their voices like crashing thunder, with them a mallet that had the people of Mina gathered over it they could not lift it. Umar said: And I upon what I am upon today? He said: And you upon what you are upon today. He said: Then I shall suffice against them, Allah willing. He said: And Ubayd ibn Umayr used to say: Yes, that is Munkar and Nakir."
"6568","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from one who narrated to him, he said: We went out with Muadh ibn Jabal to a funeral, and he said: You will not leave, while they bury him, until your companion hears the striking of your sandals, and the companion of the grave comes to him from before his head, and his tongue says: Do not come to him from before me, for he used to stand with the Book of Allah, Exalted, and toil, and this is when he rested. Then he comes to him from toward his feet, and his foot says: Do not come to him from before us, for he used to walk with us to the prayers. And he comes to him from before his right, and it says: Do not come to him from before me, for he used to extend with his right in charity. And he comes to him from before his left, and his left says: Do not come to him from before me, for he used to bear weapons upon me, or he said: in weapons in the path of Allah. And he stands from before his face and strikes him, and says: What do you say about this man? And Allah keeps him firm, and if he was doubting, he says: I do not know, I heard the people saying something. And he strikes him a blow heard by everything attending except the two weighty ones."
"6569","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Muhammad ibn Qays informed me, he said: A man came to Abu al-Darda and asked him about a verse, and he did not inform him, and the man turned away saying: Those who conceal what We sent down of clear proofs and guidance, Surah al-Baqarah verse 159. And Abu al-Darda said: How when you enter your grave and two black blue angels are brought out for you, treading upon their hair and digging with their fangs, and they ask about Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what kind of man will you be if you are firm in him? And he mentioned that with them is a mallet that had the two weighty ones gathered over it, or he said: the people of Mina, they could not bear it. How will you be when the bridge of Hell is laid, what kind of man will you be if you pass over it or are safe? And how will you be when there is of the earth naught but the place of your foot, and no shade but the shade of the Throne of the Most Merciful, what kind of man will you be when you shade yourself with it? Go to yourself, for by Allah besides whom there is no god, this is the truth."
"6570","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: For him is a straitened living. He said: His grave is narrowed over him until his ribs differ."
"6571","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered one day a palm grove of Banu al-Najjar, and heard the voices of men of Banu al-Najjar who died in Jahiliyyah being punished in their graves, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out frightened from the grave, and ordered his Companions to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave."
"6572","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Umm Khalid bint Said ibn al-As, from her mother, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."
"6573","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: This community is tried in their graves; when the believer enters his grave and his companions turn away from him, a sternly rebuking angel comes to him and says: What did you say about this man? And the believer says: I say he is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and His servant. And the angel says to him: Look at your seat that was yours of the Fire; Allah has saved you from it, and replaced for you in its place your seat that you see of Paradise. And he sees them both, and the believer says: Shall I gladden my family? And it is said to him: Be still, for this is your seat forever. And the hypocrite, when his companions turn away from him, it is said to him: What did you say about this man? And he says: I do not know, I say what the people say. And it is said to him: You did not know. Look at your seat that was yours of Paradise; Allah has replaced for you in its place your seat of the Fire."
"6574","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: When a man dies his seat is shown to him morning and evening; if he is of the people of Paradise then Paradise, and if he is of the people of the Fire then the Fire. And it is said: This is your seat whither you shall be raised on the Day of Resurrection."
"6575","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: Every servant is raised upon what he died upon, the believer upon his faith and the hypocrite upon his hypocrisy."
"6576","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, while the bier of Sad ibn Muadh was before them: The Throne of the Most Merciful shook for it."
"6577","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, and I heard Hisham ibn Hassan narrate, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves the meeting of Allah, Allah loves his meeting, and whoever hates the meeting of Allah, Allah hates his meeting. We said: O Messenger of Allah, we all hate death. He said: When Allah wills to take the believer, He uncovers for him what delights him, and thereupon he loves the meeting of Allah, and Allah loves his meeting."
"6578","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Atiyyah al-Wadii, he said: I and Masruq entered upon Aishah, and we said: Ibn Masud said: Whoever loves the meeting of Allah, Allah loves his meeting, and whoever hates the meeting of Allah, Allah hates his meeting, and death is before the meeting of Allah. And she said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Abd al-Rahman; he narrated to you a hadith whose end you did not ask him about, and I shall narrate to you about that. When Allah wills good for His servant, He sets over him an angel a year before his death, and he guides him aright and eases him until he dies in his best state, and when he attends and sees his reward of Paradise, his soul begins to heave, wishing it would come out, and thereupon he loves the meeting of Allah, and Allah loves his meeting. And when He wills evil for a servant, He sets over him a devil a year before his death, and he blocks him and misguides him and tempts him until he dies in his worst state, and people say, so-and-so died in his worst state, and when he attends and sees his reward of the Fire, his soul begins to be swallowed, wishing it would not come out, and thereupon he hates the meeting of Allah, and Allah hates his meeting."
"6579","From al-Thawri, he said: Ali said: It is forbidden for a soul to come out until it knows whether to Paradise or to the Fire."
"6580","From Mamar, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: There shall come out after you people who deny the punishment of the grave, and deny the Most Merciful, and deny the Dajjal, and deny the Hawd, and deny people who come out of the Fire."
"6581","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, he said: A man died, and when he was entered into his grave the angels came to him and said: We shall flog you a hundred lashes of the punishment of Allah. He said: And he mentioned his prayer and his fast and his striving. He said: And they lightened for him until it reached ten, then he asked them until they lightened for him until it came to one, and they said: We shall flog you one lash, there is no escape from it. And they flogged him a lash and his grave blazed with light, and he lost consciousness, and when he recovered, he said: For what did you flog me this lash? They said: One day you urinated then prayed without performing ablution, and you heard a wronged man crying for help and did not help him."
"6582","From Mamar, from Tawus, and from Qatadah also: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by two graves while upon a mule, and it shied with him, and he said: It shied, and rightly so; the owners of these two graves are being punished for no great matter or trial. As for one of them, he would not shield himself from urine, and as for the other, he would eat the flesh of people. Then he broke a palm stalk and planted one over each grave, and it was said to him: What will this avail them? And he said: Perhaps it will be lightened for them, as long as they are moist."
"6583","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by two graves and said: This is the grave of so-and-so, and this is the grave of so-and-so, and they are being punished for no great matter, yet indeed; as for one of them, he would not guard against his urine, and as for the other, he would slander people. Then he took a moist palm stalk and broke it, and placed one over this and one over this, and said: Perhaps the punishment will be lightened for them, as long as they are moist. Ibn Uyaynah said: And Mansur informed me, from Mujahid, from Tawus, the same."
"6584","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah or Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from the punishment of the grave, and from the punishment of the Fire, and from the trial of the Fire, and from the trial of the False Messiah, the Dajjal."
"6585","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from al-Walid ibn Marwan, from Tawq, a man of al-Atik, he said: Yazid ibn al-Muhallab narrated to me that he was with Sulayman and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz in the bathhouse; Sulayman was in the inner room, and I and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz were in the second room, with no one else with us. He said: And he began asking me about my bravery and I told him, and Umar said to me: O Abu Khalid, I shall narrate to you a hadith; one of them is secret and the other open. As for the secret: I had descended into the grave of al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik when they lowered him into his grave, and when we took him from his bier and placed him upon our hands, he stirred in his shrouds, and we placed him in his grave, and his son said: My father is alive, my father is alive. And I said: Your father is not alive, but they meet this in their graves. And as for the open: This one has appointed you over Iraq, so fear Allah regarding them, for they have met trial from al-Hajjaj, and met from Qutaybah ibn Muslim."
"6586","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar said: Only two men are tried, a believer and a hypocrite. As for the believer, he is tried seven, and as for the hypocrite, he is tried forty mornings, and as for the disbeliever, he is not asked about Muhammad, nor does he know him. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: It has been said in that, and we have not seen the like of a man whose dead is most sensible for seven that charity be given on his behalf."
"6587","From Ibn Jurayj, Ubayd ibn Umayr said, and he mentioned Munkar and Nakir: Fire comes out of their mouths and their eyes, and upon them are haircloths, and the earth quakes with him, until when he is separated from his reason and understands nothing with his reason except what Allah casts upon his tongue, and they say: Who is your Lord? And he mentioned the like of the hadith of Mamar."
"6588","Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Tawus said, from his father, he said: And they say to him: You did not know, nor did you prosper; woe to you, how wretched you are; you spoke truly, by Allah, upon that you lived, and upon that, by Allah, you shall die, and upon that you shall be raised, Allah willing. Woe to you, look at what Allah has turned away from you of His mercy, and look at your seat of the Fire. Then his shroud is stripped and replaced with garments of fire, and it is narrowed over him until his ribs differ in him, then an opening is opened between him and the Fire, from which its heat and its wind and its stench come out over him."
"6589","From Mamar, he said: Abu Hurayrah said: The earth eats all of the son of Adam except the tailbone, and from it he is composed, or he said: joined. He said: And he said: The earth rains a rain that sprouts the bodies of people until it becomes a body without a soul, then the soul is breathed into it."
"6590","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd ibn Umayr said: That is Munkar and Nakir; fire comes out of their mouths and their eyes, and upon them are haircloths, and the earth quakes with him, until when he is separated from his reason and understands nothing with his reason except what Allah casts upon his tongue, they say: Who is your Lord? And he says: Allah. And what is your religion? And he says: Islam. And who is your prophet? And he says: Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And they say: How do you know? Did you see him? And he says: No, but the Book of Allah came with that, and I believed in it and affirmed it. And they say: You spoke truly; upon that, by Allah, you lived, and upon that you died, and upon that you shall be raised, Allah willing. Look, may Allah have mercy on you, at what Allah has turned away from you of the Fire, and look at your seat of Paradise. Then his shroud is replaced with garments of the garments of Paradise, and his grave is widened for him as far as his eye can see, and an opening is opened between him and Paradise, from which its wind and its ease and its coolness and its fragrance enter over him. And as for the hypocrite, he is struck with the mallet a blow and sits up, and they say to him: Who is your Lord? And he says: Allah. And what is your religion? And he says: Islam. And who is your prophet? And he says: Muhammad. And they say: Why do you say that, did you see him? And he says: No, by Allah, I do not know."
"6591","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One visiting a sick person is in the harvest of Paradise until he returns."
"6592","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Visit the sick, and follow funeral biers, for they remind of the Hereafter."
"6593","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, from Abu Musa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Answer the inviter, and visit the sick, and free the captive."
"6594","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Bistam ibn Muslim, from Zinba al-Anbari, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, that Anas ibn Malik said to him: O Abu Abd Allah, we used to relate that one visiting the sick wades in mercy; if he asks after the sick standing, mercy bridles him, and if he sits, it overwhelms him."
"6595","From Mamar, from Aban, from Anas or al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever visits a sick person, and accompanies a funeral, and is granted the fast of that day, reaches evening with Paradise made binding for him."
"6596","And al-Hasan said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Which of you visited a sick person today? And Abu Bakr said: I. He said: Which of you gave charity today of his wealth? Abu Bakr said: I. He said: Which of you accompanied a funeral today? Abu Bakr said: I. He said: Which of you woke up fasting? Abu Bakr said: I. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have made binding, meaning Paradise."
"6597","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Makhul informed me that a man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How are you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Well, of a man who did not fast today, nor visit a sick person. And the man said: And what is visiting the sick, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Like fasting."
"6598","From Mamar, from Aban, from a man, he said: Ali entered upon his son al-Hasan, and with him was al-Ashari, and he said: What brought you early, O shaykh? He said: I heard of the pain of the son of my brother, and I wished to visit him. And he said: It does not prevent us, what is in ourselves, from narrating to you what we heard: Whoever visits a sick person by day, seventy thousand angels bless him until evening, and if he visits him by night, seventy thousand angels bless him until morning."
"6599","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The best of visiting is the lightest of it."
"6600","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust narrated to me that Amr ibn Hurayth visited Husayn ibn Ali, and met Ali, and Ali said to Amr: Did you visit Husayn? He said: Yes. He said: Despite what is in the breast? He said: You, O Abu Hasan, cannot expel what is in the breast. He said: That did not prevent me counsel for you: Any man who visits a sick person, seventy thousand angels are set over him blessing him until the like of it of the morrow, and if he sits, in the meadows of Paradise and in the mercy of Allah."
"6601","From Mamar, from Jabir or another, from al-Shabi, he said: I heard him say: What the family of the sick person meets of the visiting of the foolish reciters is harder than what they meet of their sick one."
"6602","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to sweat in his illness in which he died."
"6603","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: He was with a brother of his while he was dying, and his forehead began to sweat, and Alqamah laughed, and Yazid ibn Aws said to him: What makes you laugh, O Abu Shibl? He said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud say: The soul of the believer comes out with sweat, and the soul of the disbeliever comes out of his cheek, as the soul of the donkey comes out. The believer is made severe upon at his death for an evil he did, to be thereby, and the disbeliever is made easy upon at his death for a good he did, to be thereby."
"6604","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Messenger of Allah, a man whom we deemed righteous falls ill, and it is made severe upon him at his death, and a man in whom we saw no good falls ill, and it is made easy upon him at his death. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer has something of his sins remaining, so it is made severe upon him at his death, that he may meet Allah with no sin upon him, and the hypocrite has something of his good deeds remaining, so it is made easy upon him, that he may meet Allah with no good deed for him. Al-Thawri said: It reached us that the treatment of the Angel of Death is severer than a thousand strikes with the sword."
"6605","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Ibn Abbas used to narrate that Abu Bakr came to the house in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, while he was in the house of Aishah, and uncovered from his face a striped Yemeni cloak, and he had been covered with it, and looked at the face of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then bent over him and kissed him, then said: By Allah, Allah shall not combine upon you two deaths; you have died the death after which there is no death."
"6606","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Uthman ibn Mazun while he was dead, and bent over him and kissed him, then wept until I saw tears flowing upon his cheeks."
"6607","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from a man, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: Sudden death is a lightening upon the believer, and regret upon the disbelievers."
"6608","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: The believer dies in every state."
"6609","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Sad ibn Ubadah stood to urinate then returned, and said: I feel something in my back, and he did not tarry but that he died, and the jinn mourned him and said: We killed the chief of Khazraj, Sad ibn Ubadah, with two arrows, and we did not miss his heart."
"6610","From Umar ibn Rashid, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Abi Kathir mention that Hudhayfah used to be severe about sudden death, a seizure upon displeasure."
"6611","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamadhani, from al-Hawari ibn Ziyad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of the nearness of the Hour is when paralysis abounds, and sudden death."
"6612","And Habib informed me, from al-Hawari ibn Ziyad, from Anas ibn Malik: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paralysis shall spread among people until it is thought to be plague."
"6613","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Ibn Sabit, from Hafsah bint Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Sudden death is a lightening upon the believer, and a seizure of regret upon the disbelievers."
"6614","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-three years, and the Quran was sent down upon him, of that at Makkah ten, and at Madinah ten. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib the same. He said: And Abu Bakr died the same."
"6615","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Revelation descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was forty, and he stayed at Makkah thirteen, and at Madinah ten, and died at sixty-three."
"6616","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, he said: Mikail was entrusted with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was forty, three years teaching the means of prophethood, and when he was forty-three Jibril was entrusted with him, and the Quran descended upon him at Makkah ten years, and at Madinah ten years, then he died at sixty-three, and Umar died at sixty-three."
"6617","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was neither tall nor short, neither curly-haired nor lank-haired, neither dark nor white. Revelation descended upon him when he was forty; he stayed at Makkah ten, and at Madinah ten, and was taken at sixty years, and there were not on his head nor in his beard twenty white hairs."
"6618","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I asked Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: How long did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stay at Makkah? He said: Ten years. He said: I said: Ibn Abbas said: Some ten. He said: He lied; he only took it from the saying of the poet. Amr ibn Dinar said: And I hated Urwah when he belied him."
"6619","From Mamar, he said: An informant informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ali that Ali died at sixty-five."
"6620","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his son Ali, that Ali was killed at fifty-eight."
"6621","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Huwayrith, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-five years, and Abu Bakr at his station, and Umar ibn al-Khattab at fifty-six, and Uthman at sixty-one."
"6622","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at the head of sixty-three. Ibn Shihab said: And Aishah said: And Abu Bakr died at the head of sixty-three. Ibn Shihab said: And Umar died at the head of fifty-five."
"6623","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, on the charity of sheep: In every forty sheep a sheep, until they reach a hundred and twenty; when one more, therein two sheep until they reach two hundred; when one more, therein three sheep to three hundred; and if sheep abound, in every hundred sheep a sheep. And in camels, in every five a sheep, and in ten two sheep, and in fifteen three sheep, and in twenty four sheep; when they reach twenty-four, therein a she-camel in her second year; if there is no she-camel in her second year, then a male in his second year, to thirty-five; if more, therein a she-camel in her third year to forty-five; if more, therein a hiqqah bred by the stallion to sixty; if more, therein a jadhaah to seventy-five; if more, therein two she-camels in their third year to ninety; if more, therein two hiqqahs bred by the stallion to a hundred and twenty; if more, in every fifty a hiqqah, and in every forty a she-camel in her third year. And their young and old are counted."
"6624","From Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for them a writing therein: In the nose when completely cut off, a hundred camels; the penetrating wound a third of a life; the wound reaching the brain the same; the eye fifty; the hand fifty; the foot fifty; and in every finger thereof of the fingers of the hands and the feet ten; the tooth five; the wound laying bare the bone five. And in sheep, in forty to a hundred and twenty a sheep; when they exceed to reach two hundred, two sheep; when they exceed two hundred to reach three hundred, therein three sheep; when they reach more than that, count in every hundred sheep. And in camels, when twenty-five to thirty-five, therein a she-camel in her second year; if no she-camel in her second year is found in the camels, then a male in his second year; when thirty-six to forty-five, therein a she-camel in her third year; when forty-six to sixty, therein a hiqqah; when more than that to seventy-five, therein a jadhaah; when more than that to ninety, therein two she-camels in their third year; when more than that to a hundred and twenty, therein two hiqqahs; when more than that, count in every fifty a hiqqah. And what is less than twenty-five, in every five a sheep; therein is no aged nor defective of sheep. And in cattle, in every thirty a tabii, and in every forty a musinnah."
"6625","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: In the nose the full blood-money, and in the glans the full blood-money, and in the tongue the full blood-money, and in the hand half the blood-money, and in the foot half the blood-money, and in the tooth five camels, and in the wound laying bare the bone five camels, and in the bone-breaking wound fifteen camels, and in the wound reaching the brain a third of the blood-money, and in the penetrating wound a third of the blood-money, and in every finger ten camels. And in five camels a sheep, and in every ten two sheep, and in fifteen three sheep, and in every twenty four sheep, and in twenty-five five sheep, and in twenty-six a she-camel in her second year; if there is no she-camel in her second year, then a male in his second year, until they reach thirty-five; when one more, therein a she-camel in her third year until they reach forty-five; when one more, therein a hiqqah bred by the stallion, or he said: the camel, until they reach sixty; when one more, therein a jadhaah until they reach seventy-five; when one more, therein two she-camels in their third year until they reach ninety; when one more, therein two hiqqahs bred by the stallion to a hundred and twenty; when one more, in every fifty a hiqqah, and in every forty a she-camel in her third year. And in cattle, in every thirty cows a yearling tabii, and in every forty cows a musinnah. And in sheep, in every forty sheep a sheep; in less than forty nothing until they reach a hundred and twenty; if one more, therein two sheep to two hundred; if one more, therein three sheep to three hundred; when more, in every hundred a sheep. And no aged nor defective is taken except that the collector wills, and there is no combining between separated nor separating between combined, and in what the sky and wells water a tenth, and in what is watered by the bucket half a tenth, and in silver when a year passes over it, in every two hundred dirhams five dirhams; in less than two hundred dirhams nothing; if more, by reckoning of that. And I have pardoned the charity of horses and slaves."
"6626","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah informed me, he said: Abu Yala Mundhir al-Thawri informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, he said: Some people came to my father and complained of the collectors of Uthman, and my father said: Take this writing and go to Uthman ibn Affan, and say to him: My father said: Some people have come complaining of your collectors, and this is the order of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the obligatory shares, so let them take by it. And I went with the writing until I entered upon Uthman, and said to him: My father sent me to you, and mentioned that some people complained of your collectors, and this is the order of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the obligatory shares, so order them to take by it. And he said: We have no need of your writing. He said: And I returned to my father and informed him, and my father said: No harm upon you, return the writing whence you took it. He said: Had he mentioned Uthman with anything he would have mentioned him, meaning with evil. He said: And in the writing was only what is in the hadith of Ali."
"6627","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: In less than forty of sheep nothing; and in forty sheep a sheep, to a hundred and twenty; when more, therein two sheep to two hundred; if more, therein three sheep to three hundred; and if sheep abound, in every hundred a sheep. No aged nor defective nor billy-goat is taken, except that the collector wills. And there is no combining between separated, nor separating between combined, for fear of charity. From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, the like of this hadith, except that Ibrahim did not mention aged nor defective nor billy-goat. Sufyan said: This is on pasturing ones; if they were for trade, we valued them a just value, and when they reach two hundred dirhams, therein is zakat."
"6628","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: In forty of sheep pasturing, a sheep to a hundred and twenty; if one sheep more, therein two sheep to two hundred; if one sheep more, therein three sheep to three hundred; and if sheep abound, in every hundred a sheep. And no aged nor defective nor billy-goat is taken, except that the collector wills. And in camels, in five a sheep, and in ten two sheep, and in fifteen three sheep, and in twenty four sheep, and in twenty-five a she-camel in her second year; if there is no she-camel in her second year, then a male in his second year to thirty-five; if one more, therein a she-camel in her third year to forty-five; if one more, therein a hiqqah bred by the stallion to sixty; if one more, therein a jadhaah to seventy-five; if one more, therein two she-camels in their third year to ninety; if one more, therein two hiqqahs bred by the stallion to a hundred and twenty; if more, in every forty a she-camel in her third year, and in every fifty a hiqqah. And their young and old are counted. And whatever is of two partners, they settle equally, and there is no separating between combined, nor combining between separated, for fear of charity. From al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, on sheep the same. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar the same. From al-Awzai, from Ubayd Allah and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar on camels the same."
"6629","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me that Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to him with a writing on charity he copied for him; Abu Bakr claimed, from a leaf he found tied to the scabbard of Umar ibn al-Khattab: In twenty-four camels, and below that of camels, in every five a sheep. And in above that to thirty-five, a she-camel in her second year; if there is no she-camel in her second year, then a male in his second year. And in above that, the like of the hadith of al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"6630","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: In less than five camels nothing; and in five a sheep, and in ten two sheep, and in fifteen three sheep, and in twenty four sheep, and in twenty-five a she-camel in her second year to thirty-five; if there is no she-camel in her second year, then a male in his second year; if one more, therein a she-camel in her third year to forty-five; if one more, therein a hiqqah bred by the stallion to sixty; if one more, therein a jadhaah to seventy-five; if one more, therein two she-camels in their third year to ninety; if one more, therein two hiqqahs bred by the stallion to a hundred and twenty; if more, resume the shares anew; when they reach fifty, in every fifty a hiqqah. Sufyan said: The explanation of our hadith from Ibrahim: When they exceed a hundred and twenty, in every five a sheep, and in every ten two sheep, and in fifteen three sheep, and in every twenty four sheep; when they reach a hundred and forty, therein two hiqqahs and four of sheep; when they reach a hundred and forty-five, therein two hiqqahs and a she-camel in her second year, meaning until they reach fifty, then therein three hiqqahs; when they exceed, you resume the shares anew as you resumed at first."
"6631","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sad ibn Said, the brother of Yahya ibn Said, informed me, he said: It kept being narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no combining between separated, nor separating between combined."
"6632","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The jadha is not taken in charity, meaning that which is weaned from its mother."
"6633","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim ibn Yanaq, that Umar ibn al-Khattab sent Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi as collector, and saw him after days in the mosque, and said to him: Are you not pleased to be as the warrior in the path of Allah? He said: And how can I have that, while they claim we wrong them? He said: What do they say? He said: They say: Is the kid counted against us? And Umar said: Count it, though the shepherd brings it carrying it upon his palm, and say to them: We leave the eater, and the nursing, and the pregnant, and the stallion. He said: And Abdullah ibn Kathir informed me, from Asim, something like this, from Umar, except that he said: Take what is between the thaniyyah to the jadhaah. He said: That is just between its poor and its choice. And the eater is the barren fat sheep, and the nursing is that which the shepherd raises."
"6634","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrimah, he said: When a man has sheep and goats, and only a sheep is due therein, the collector takes from the more numerous of the two kinds."
"6635","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Bishr ibn Asim ibn Sufyan informed me, that Asim ibn Sufyan narrated to them, that Sufyan ibn Abd Allah, while collecting in the districts of al-Taif, the owners of livestock complained to him of counting the young, and said: If you count young, take its charity from it. And Sufyan returned nothing to them until he met Umar ibn al-Khattab, and said: The owners of livestock complain to me that I count the young, and they say: If you count it, take its charity from it. He said: Say to them: We count all the young, even the kid the shepherd brings upon his hand. He said: And he said: I do not take therein the eater, nor the ram of sheep, nor the nursing, nor the pregnant, but I take the young she-goat, and the jadhaah, and the thaniyyah, and that is just between the young and the choice of wealth. And say to them: We count all the young, even the kid. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The young is counted."
"6636","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, he said: Sheep are split in two halves; the owner of the sheep chooses one of them, and the collector chooses from the other kind."
"6637","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: It is divided three kinds; the owner of the sheep chooses its best, and the collector takes from the middle."
"6638","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, he said: Umar said on the charity of sheep: He selects them, meaning its owner selects them sheep by sheep until he sets aside a third of them, then he splits the sheep in two halves, and the collector chooses from one of them."
"6639","From Mamar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, from Shihab ibn Abd al-Malik, from Sad al-Araj, that Umar ibn al-Khattab met Sad and said: Where do you intend? And he said: To raid. And Umar said to him: Return to your companion, meaning Yala ibn Umayyah, for work with truth is fine striving. And when you collect livestock, do not forget the good, nor make its owner forget it. Then divide them three, then the owner of the sheep chooses a third, then choose from the remaining two thirds. Sad said: And we used to go out collecting then return with naught but our whips. Mamar said: Meaning they divide them."
"6640","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a collector and said: Take the aged, and the tooth-worn, and the one-eyed. He said: And I do not know but that he said: Then the obligatory shares came after."
"6641","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf employed Tawus over Hakam to collect their wealth. He said: And he collected it then did not return with him a dirham. He said: I said to him: How did you do, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: We would stand over a man among his family and wealth, and say: Give charity, may Allah have mercy on you, of what Allah gave you. And if he brought out to us what we deemed the due, we accepted; else we said to him: Seek the good pleasure, may Allah have mercy on you. And if he did, else we accepted from him what he gave us. Then we looked to the neediest household and handed it to them. He said: I said to him: If a man came to you with his charity and stood over you with it then returned with it, he said: Then we would not return it."
"6642","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi, that he came to Umar, and he had employed him over al-Taif, and said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, the owners of livestock claim that we count against them the young and do not take it. He said: Count against them, but do not take them, even the kid the shepherd brings upon his hands, and say to them: We leave the nursing, and the ram of sheep, and the pregnant, and the meat-sheep, and take of the young she-goat, and it is the term between us and you. The nursing is whose young is with it walking, and the pregnant is in whose belly is its young. He said: Then Safwan ibn Umayyah sent to him a bowl of meat carried by a group, and it was placed with Umar, and that in the Sacred Mosque. He said: Then the people who carried it withdrew, and Umar said to them: Draw near; may Allah fight people who turn away from these. And a speaker said: O Commander of the Faithful, they do not turn away from them, but they prefer others over them. He said: And it was lighter with him. He said: Then Abu Mahdhurah called the adhan, and Umar said: Did you not fear your uvula would be torn? He said: I wished to make you hear, O Commander of the Faithful. And Umar said: Your land, O company of the people of Tihamah, is hot, so cool, then cool, then call the adhan. Then your comer shall repeat. Then he entered upon Safwan ibn Umayyah his house, and they had covered it with figured leather, and Umar said: Had you placed in place of this haircloth, it would have borne dust better than this."
"6643","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard my father and others mention that Umar wrote on sheep that they be divided thirds, then its master chooses a third, and the collector chooses his due from the middle third."
"6644","Mamar informed us, he said: A shaykh of Banu Sadus called Daysam informed me, from Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyyah, and he had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he named him Bashir. He said: We came to him and said: The collectors count against us; shall we conceal from them the measure of what they add over us? He said: No, but gather them, and when they take them, order them to bless you. Then he recited: And bless them; your blessing is a tranquillity for them. He said: We said: We have neighbours of Banu Tamim; no sheep strays of ours but that they take it, and things of their wealth are hidden from us; shall we take them? He said: No."
"6645","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: It reached us that charity is in livestock in the middle third of wealth. He said: And if they were camels, the due ones chosen of charity are brought out; the master of the livestock chooses a due one, and the collector chooses a due one until the collector completes his due. And if they were cattle, a cow is taken from the middle of the wealth, aged or in her second year and above. And if they were sheep, the sheep are divided three thirds; the master of the wealth chooses a third, and the collector chooses of the third next to it his due."
"6646","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: I reached, and was informed, that whatever the owner of wealth brings out, they accepted it, of all livestock, and he does not bring out young, nor male, nor defective, nor aged."
"6647","From Muhammad ibn Muslim and others, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from a man he named and I forgot, he said: I asked Abu Hurayrah in which wealth is charity? He said: In the middle third. And when the collector comes to you, bring out for him the middle third, the jadhaah and the thaniyyah. He said: If he takes, it is his due, and if he refuses, do not withhold from him, nor revile him, and feed him of your food, and say to him a kind word."
"6648","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from some of the Ansar that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to one of his workers a writing enjoining upon him: Take charity from the Muslims as purification of their deeds, and zakat of their wealth, and a ruling of the rulings of Allah. Transgression therein is injustice and wrong to the Muslims, and falling short of it is flattery in the truth and betrayal of the trust. So call people with their wealth to the gentlest gathering places, and nearest to their interests, and do not detain people, their first for their last, for the plague is severe upon livestock, upon it are delays. And do not drive them a driving that removes pasture and water from them. And when a man halts his sheep over you, do not take the pick of his sheep, nor take of its lowest, and take charity of its middle. And do not take from a man, if you do not find in his camels the age due upon him, but that age of like his camels, or a just value. And look at those with milk and the pregnant of what charity is due from, and turn away from them for the interests of the Muslims, for they are the wealth of their present, and the provision of their absent or traveller, and the store of their time. Then divide to the poor, and begin with the weak needy, and the orphans, and the widows, and the elders. And whoever gathers to you of the needy, and they were a household taking turns and bearing together, divide to them what was of camels they take turns its bearing, and if of sheep grant them. And whoever was alone, do not lessen each five of them of a share or ten anything to fifteen of sheep."
"6649","From Mamar, from a man, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When the collector comes to you, say: This is my wealth, and this is my charity. And if he is pleased, else turn your face from him, and leave him and what he does, and do not curse him."
"6650","From Mamar, from Bahz ibn Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: In every forty pasturing camels a she-camel in her third year. Whoever gives it seeking reward, for him is its reward, and whoever conceals it, we shall take it and half his camels, a binding decree of the decrees of your Lord. It is not lawful for Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor for the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6651","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to take a fifth of the wealth of one whose wealth was hidden from charity."
"6652","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A hadith was narrated raised to Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged people on charity, and he was brought, and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfah, and Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Abbas, the uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), have withheld charity. And he said: What does Ibn Jamil resent of us but that he was poor, and Allah and His Messenger enriched him. And as for Khalid ibn al-Walid, he has kept his coats of mail and counted them in the path of Allah. And as for Abbas, the uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is upon him and the like of it with it."
"6653","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there charity in pack animals and plough animals? And he said: No. And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: We heard that. And Abd al-Karim said: Thus we say: There is no charity in pack animals, nor plough animals, and he did not trace it from anyone."
"6654","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: There is no charity in plough animals."
"6655","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: There is no charity on working cattle."
